[{:db/id 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b79622ff-7b5d-5073-9074-7493234a4c4c" "question-d267d682-18e1-5263-9cb3-12f52098c9f0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b79622ff-7b5d-5073-9074-7493234a4c4c" :question/id #uuid "b79622ff-7b5d-5073-9074-7493234a4c4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80541" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and $y=3.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/1c/1ca0b430b798a6dc85fd2b731a93690f79bb2c45b33bbe6f31a5618a2ce0888a.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-af4d3735-879f-5545-9728-93257a5ef240"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-af4d3735-879f-5545-9728-93257a5ef240" :answer-field/id #uuid "af4d3735-879f-5545-9728-93257a5ef240" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e34df759-bc0a-51b2-ad62-1b6a95b769d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e34df759-bc0a-51b2-ad62-1b6a95b769d5" "answer-082aacc4-affa-5540-8731-03749c801007" "answer-95d04760-a4c8-5276-a4e8-6b9eb0e6763a" "answer-f7a0c571-503d-5fe4-884d-519e3a27a77e" "answer-418a7860-4a6f-515d-a959-667a76f75c87"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e34df759-bc0a-51b2-ad62-1b6a95b769d5" :answer/id #uuid "e34df759-bc0a-51b2-ad62-1b6a95b769d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-082aacc4-affa-5540-8731-03749c801007" :answer/id #uuid "082aacc4-affa-5540-8731-03749c801007" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95d04760-a4c8-5276-a4e8-6b9eb0e6763a" :answer/id #uuid "95d04760-a4c8-5276-a4e8-6b9eb0e6763a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7a0c571-503d-5fe4-884d-519e3a27a77e" :answer/id #uuid "f7a0c571-503d-5fe4-884d-519e3a27a77e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-418a7860-4a6f-515d-a959-667a76f75c87" :answer/id #uuid "418a7860-4a6f-515d-a959-667a76f75c87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d267d682-18e1-5263-9cb3-12f52098c9f0" :question/id #uuid "d267d682-18e1-5263-9cb3-12f52098c9f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86895" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f5f6dae762414eb327b9eae9e5445ef1916e9efc0e17c1316339bbeb416ec94e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ y-x, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R∖D, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/97/976dc1b0a7298c5cf0a331acb00594c8e4c74a14b6df3fc18f7e7997db2956c4.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $y-x$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $y-x$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6e63f712-8c7c-524e-90a0-bc57875864f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6e63f712-8c7c-524e-90a0-bc57875864f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "6e63f712-8c7c-524e-90a0-bc57875864f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16817ae5-5f38-5dd6-a4f9-252084ee3df2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-040b0f42-7c9b-56eb-b6be-621b67822865" "answer-d093b965-6d59-5136-ba07-f06c5e2040b5" "answer-0e4b6a27-3956-5728-98e5-4951488d4928" "answer-16817ae5-5f38-5dd6-a4f9-252084ee3df2" "answer-2cb42234-9f6f-50f6-b70b-14633db426c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-040b0f42-7c9b-56eb-b6be-621b67822865" :answer/id #uuid "040b0f42-7c9b-56eb-b6be-621b67822865" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d093b965-6d59-5136-ba07-f06c5e2040b5" :answer/id #uuid "d093b965-6d59-5136-ba07-f06c5e2040b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e4b6a27-3956-5728-98e5-4951488d4928" :answer/id #uuid "0e4b6a27-3956-5728-98e5-4951488d4928" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16817ae5-5f38-5dd6-a4f9-252084ee3df2" :answer/id #uuid "16817ae5-5f38-5dd6-a4f9-252084ee3df2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cb42234-9f6f-50f6-b70b-14633db426c1" :answer/id #uuid "2cb42234-9f6f-50f6-b70b-14633db426c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-0c2621c9-fca8-5e71-80e1-32bcc7c2b629" :activity/id #uuid "0c2621c9-fca8-5e71-80e1-32bcc7c2b629" :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049229 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-1b5af6dc-d98b-5c64-b233-11f3ee5e3995" "step-62090604-96b8-5d41-8a4a-a6c72e1082c9" "step-3cccf520-8b64-5626-8769-b89d045f3205" "step-186a0fbc-5b23-5be4-8814-746a9a9a5c7c"] :activity/first-step "step-1b5af6dc-d98b-5c64-b233-11f3ee5e3995" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1b5af6dc-d98b-5c64-b233-11f3ee5e3995" :step/id #uuid "1b5af6dc-d98b-5c64-b233-11f3ee5e3995" :step/content "question-b79622ff-7b5d-5073-9074-7493234a4c4c" :step/next "step-62090604-96b8-5d41-8a4a-a6c72e1082c9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-62090604-96b8-5d41-8a4a-a6c72e1082c9" :step/id #uuid "62090604-96b8-5d41-8a4a-a6c72e1082c9" :step/content 17592186348413 :step/next "step-3cccf520-8b64-5626-8769-b89d045f3205" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3cccf520-8b64-5626-8769-b89d045f3205" :step/id #uuid "3cccf520-8b64-5626-8769-b89d045f3205" :step/content 17592186425929 :step/next "step-186a0fbc-5b23-5be4-8814-746a9a9a5c7c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-186a0fbc-5b23-5be4-8814-746a9a9a5c7c" :step/id #uuid "186a0fbc-5b23-5be4-8814-746a9a9a5c7c" :step/content "question-d267d682-18e1-5263-9cb3-12f52098c9f0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186197648 :answer-field/choices 17592186197649] [:db/retract 17592186197648 :answer-field/choices 17592186197650] [:db/retract 17592186197648 :answer-field/choices 17592186197651] [:db/retract 17592186197648 :answer-field/choices 17592186197652] [:db/retract 17592186197648 :answer-field/choices 17592186197653] [:db/retract 17592186197654 :answer-field/choices 17592186197657] [:db/retract 17592186197654 :answer-field/choices 17592186197659] {:db/id 17592186198397 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-22a02818-7cda-52f6-bd03-f172a29927f5" "question-15e16006-8a30-5d22-b639-4bcd0e964462" "question-4f86e12d-91a6-559a-805d-f5f0798ca229" "question-46b1b539-1890-56ee-b628-8f298fa77da5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198039 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/b0/b072e1c4e9b4eee6d79c46996af36a570696aa43b52009aed4c0242bbdbc174b.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,  \\qquad\nv=y, \\qquad\nw=2z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/43/43eda6a90b6650ff4e9fe8b73bb22126201c860e33060a256ff71761ff1530be.svg)\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(y-0) \\\\  & =2y \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}3x{y}^{2}+6yz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(3uv+3vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}3v(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{2}(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198398 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e662cc33-8ce5-5a82-9731-097b3595e6c3" "question-d8c9e8ae-5eea-50e8-acfe-85f90d5a9922" "question-3b7c4644-c6ca-51e8-b34c-e72d6ef6b667"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198040 :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c7/c71a8a9cf964b3c0a6123c616da0eed54a2a8dcfae2ca2bb6f83bb135d008442.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad\nv=  y+z, \\qquad\nw = 2z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/68/68ee2a9eee49a479542cf9b1c6342b14d313fa2ad3c6e82f38bc4e584d7f15e2.svg)\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 4,\\,0\\le w\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{15}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}\\cdot \\frac{w}{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{15}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{15}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{16}}{16}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=4}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{16}-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198399 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b4b2886a-abaf-53c5-8dbb-583291c4a37e" "question-fd1760e0-c4d7-5ae6-945d-3f3e267b54eb" "question-7144ca6a-debb-5e59-baf7-8232b8798b6c" "question-f69775da-3c2e-512a-8912-f342f7d0a00f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198041 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/8a/8a5074508ee964b1d8a8e06748967bb8cbfa4be838a9b60df643e14f2534057c.svg)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x+y \\leq 1, \\qquad\n-2 \\leq y-2x \\leq 2, \\qquad\n1 \\leq z^3 \\leq 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y, \\qquad\nv = y-2x, \\qquad\nw = z^3.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/bf/bf84bc5921637c5f5c7fda7b8cb549c993444a6eb1e2df6468e22c3ed041fdd7.svg)\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-2\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac 19 w^{-2/3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{9}{w}^{-2/3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{9}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [v]_{-2}^{2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{1}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-(-2))\\cdot 3({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-22a02818-7cda-52f6-bd03-f172a29927f5" :question/id #uuid "22a02818-7cda-52f6-bd03-f172a29927f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161892" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le xy\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le xy+3\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3,\\,-v\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ -y & -x & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\  & =-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v(w+v)\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d68eca02-5016-5081-b3c3-150bae83f3f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d68eca02-5016-5081-b3c3-150bae83f3f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "d68eca02-5016-5081-b3c3-150bae83f3f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e72adb6e-7faa-57b5-8126-d2e42feeb89a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-524eda41-ddb8-5f48-ab3e-43c84e18bcd6" "answer-f97b8fcd-b3a2-520e-9d92-adc67193b681" "answer-e72adb6e-7faa-57b5-8126-d2e42feeb89a" "answer-cbe036c6-1bc2-5a04-8662-ddfdd4bc5170" "answer-8c235bdb-c968-5d76-8236-6fd1197822e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-524eda41-ddb8-5f48-ab3e-43c84e18bcd6" :answer/id #uuid "524eda41-ddb8-5f48-ab3e-43c84e18bcd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-uv}^{3}u(w+v)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f97b8fcd-b3a2-520e-9d92-adc67193b681" :answer/id #uuid "f97b8fcd-b3a2-520e-9d92-adc67193b681" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{u(w+v)}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e72adb6e-7faa-57b5-8126-d2e42feeb89a" :answer/id #uuid "e72adb6e-7faa-57b5-8126-d2e42feeb89a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbe036c6-1bc2-5a04-8662-ddfdd4bc5170" :answer/id #uuid "cbe036c6-1bc2-5a04-8662-ddfdd4bc5170" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{u+v}^{2}u(w+v)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c235bdb-c968-5d76-8236-6fd1197822e0" :answer/id #uuid "8c235bdb-c968-5d76-8236-6fd1197822e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{u-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-15e16006-8a30-5d22-b639-4bcd0e964462" :question/id #uuid "15e16006-8a30-5d22-b639-4bcd0e964462" :question/math-academy-id "q-39058" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps: \n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e977d9ba-fc00-5472-b57e-d5737f833b3e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e977d9ba-fc00-5472-b57e-d5737f833b3e" :answer-field/id #uuid "e977d9ba-fc00-5472-b57e-d5737f833b3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ee553bba-00ce-56b8-8960-df4b7c0e9646" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8685f08f-d476-5822-b98a-1d7e181d364e" "answer-abcdf334-5232-5108-815d-8544de96b5b1" "answer-b73ed2ab-45c7-58bf-8285-14105b905660" "answer-acad01e2-2ec4-510b-8062-e56c2b02423d" "answer-ee553bba-00ce-56b8-8960-df4b7c0e9646"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8685f08f-d476-5822-b98a-1d7e181d364e" :answer/id #uuid "8685f08f-d476-5822-b98a-1d7e181d364e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abcdf334-5232-5108-815d-8544de96b5b1" :answer/id #uuid "abcdf334-5232-5108-815d-8544de96b5b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b73ed2ab-45c7-58bf-8285-14105b905660" :answer/id #uuid "b73ed2ab-45c7-58bf-8285-14105b905660" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acad01e2-2ec4-510b-8062-e56c2b02423d" :answer/id #uuid "acad01e2-2ec4-510b-8062-e56c2b02423d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee553bba-00ce-56b8-8960-df4b7c0e9646" :answer/id #uuid "ee553bba-00ce-56b8-8960-df4b7c0e9646" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f86e12d-91a6-559a-805d-f5f0798ca229" :question/id #uuid "4f86e12d-91a6-559a-805d-f5f0798ca229" :question/math-academy-id "q-161889" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le x-y\\le 3,\\,1\\le {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4,\\,3\\le w\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(-2y+2x) \\\\  & =6(x-y) \\\\  & =6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}2w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{6u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-976640a1-1292-535e-95ff-f6793512061b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-976640a1-1292-535e-95ff-f6793512061b" :answer-field/id #uuid "976640a1-1292-535e-95ff-f6793512061b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-47bf8ad5-d857-5c65-9120-529840b5d37f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa08f136-85ba-53cf-9533-f666c9d80525" "answer-f069b42f-d4b1-545c-b713-4a2714ba1128" "answer-0eca66db-cc53-5a4d-aca7-eaf9a7ab3b31" "answer-47bf8ad5-d857-5c65-9120-529840b5d37f" "answer-d8a29ad8-5f0f-54a0-a2fe-7f5f6a9c57e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa08f136-85ba-53cf-9533-f666c9d80525" :answer/id #uuid "fa08f136-85ba-53cf-9533-f666c9d80525" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2w}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{6u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f069b42f-d4b1-545c-b713-4a2714ba1128" :answer/id #uuid "f069b42f-d4b1-545c-b713-4a2714ba1128" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3w}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{6}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0eca66db-cc53-5a4d-aca7-eaf9a7ab3b31" :answer/id #uuid "0eca66db-cc53-5a4d-aca7-eaf9a7ab3b31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{2}^{2w}w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47bf8ad5-d857-5c65-9120-529840b5d37f" :answer/id #uuid "47bf8ad5-d857-5c65-9120-529840b5d37f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8a29ad8-5f0f-54a0-a2fe-7f5f6a9c57e2" :answer/id #uuid "d8a29ad8-5f0f-54a0-a2fe-7f5f6a9c57e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{w}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡v}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198963 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le 2x+y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le u\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186197648 :answer-field/correct "answer-8da2e5ae-0945-59a5-8ed1-21ba6659c06f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97ba92a3-dcbc-53fc-b77a-c4ddb79e17c9" "answer-118c0a6d-2d86-5402-ab17-e9a5a70e791e" "answer-8da2e5ae-0945-59a5-8ed1-21ba6659c06f" "answer-feda8ec9-58b4-5dda-999b-6d6a16d95a6a" "answer-1a807e54-a4b6-5ff5-bbcc-a7cd1282fba2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97ba92a3-dcbc-53fc-b77a-c4ddb79e17c9" :answer/id #uuid "97ba92a3-dcbc-53fc-b77a-c4ddb79e17c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-118c0a6d-2d86-5402-ab17-e9a5a70e791e" :answer/id #uuid "118c0a6d-2d86-5402-ab17-e9a5a70e791e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8da2e5ae-0945-59a5-8ed1-21ba6659c06f" :answer/id #uuid "8da2e5ae-0945-59a5-8ed1-21ba6659c06f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-feda8ec9-58b4-5dda-999b-6d6a16d95a6a" :answer/id #uuid "feda8ec9-58b4-5dda-999b-6d6a16d95a6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a807e54-a4b6-5ff5-bbcc-a7cd1282fba2" :answer/id #uuid "1a807e54-a4b6-5ff5-bbcc-a7cd1282fba2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-46b1b539-1890-56ee-b628-8f298fa77da5" :question/id #uuid "46b1b539-1890-56ee-b628-8f298fa77da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161871" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le xy\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(x-0) \\\\  & =3x \\\\  & =3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-176c3a3c-6656-5311-869f-fdb6f745be21"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-176c3a3c-6656-5311-869f-fdb6f745be21" :answer-field/id #uuid "176c3a3c-6656-5311-869f-fdb6f745be21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e4dfc26b-d49c-54ee-9547-1ec07e222142" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0a545db6-b2c1-5af7-bab8-a92a067e183d" "answer-6c9f7ec3-cf1f-5f6e-8b5d-a4981ae3628c" "answer-ad6f11e6-c760-5ae7-8acd-14a00b864038" "answer-e4dfc26b-d49c-54ee-9547-1ec07e222142" "answer-68e6ba86-dd63-5072-80c7-8937c30e291d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a545db6-b2c1-5af7-bab8-a92a067e183d" :answer/id #uuid "0a545db6-b2c1-5af7-bab8-a92a067e183d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3w}\\frac{1}{3}(u+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c9f7ec3-cf1f-5f6e-8b5d-a4981ae3628c" :answer/id #uuid "6c9f7ec3-cf1f-5f6e-8b5d-a4981ae3628c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3w}\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad6f11e6-c760-5ae7-8acd-14a00b864038" :answer/id #uuid "ad6f11e6-c760-5ae7-8acd-14a00b864038" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{u}{3}+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4dfc26b-d49c-54ee-9547-1ec07e222142" :answer/id #uuid "e4dfc26b-d49c-54ee-9547-1ec07e222142" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68e6ba86-dd63-5072-80c7-8937c30e291d" :answer/id #uuid "68e6ba86-dd63-5072-80c7-8937c30e291d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{v}(\\frac{w}{u}+3vu)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e662cc33-8ce5-5a82-9731-097b3595e6c3" :question/id #uuid "e662cc33-8ce5-5a82-9731-097b3595e6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162140" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le y+z\\le 5,\\,0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 5,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{9}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{9}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{10}}{10}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=5}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{10}-0)\\cdot (5-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3587c6f6-e4fb-567f-a4f3-80de70f97f45"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3587c6f6-e4fb-567f-a4f3-80de70f97f45" :answer-field/id #uuid "3587c6f6-e4fb-567f-a4f3-80de70f97f45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1ee0c1d0-b902-5d6e-9fd8-7baec2f61f9c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1ee0c1d0-b902-5d6e-9fd8-7baec2f61f9c" "answer-70ef93b1-e501-54d3-bcff-1f06883cd750" "answer-572c02e1-9d71-5b99-960a-6f12882be763" "answer-75475609-dfeb-560d-bc22-9379064434fc" "answer-8e5aafc8-ceba-5678-a412-98f1f904e581"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ee0c1d0-b902-5d6e-9fd8-7baec2f61f9c" :answer/id #uuid "1ee0c1d0-b902-5d6e-9fd8-7baec2f61f9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70ef93b1-e501-54d3-bcff-1f06883cd750" :answer/id #uuid "70ef93b1-e501-54d3-bcff-1f06883cd750" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-572c02e1-9d71-5b99-960a-6f12882be763" :answer/id #uuid "572c02e1-9d71-5b99-960a-6f12882be763" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75475609-dfeb-560d-bc22-9379064434fc" :answer/id #uuid "75475609-dfeb-560d-bc22-9379064434fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e5aafc8-ceba-5678-a412-98f1f904e581" :answer/id #uuid "8e5aafc8-ceba-5678-a412-98f1f904e581" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d8c9e8ae-5eea-50e8-acfe-85f90d5a9922" :question/id #uuid "d8c9e8ae-5eea-50e8-acfe-85f90d5a9922" :question/math-academy-id "q-161933" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\,\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y+z\\le 2,\\,1\\le x+4z\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le e,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-1)(-1). \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{v=1}^{v=e}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0305ffe-7471-5805-bce3-ea016f3e92ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0305ffe-7471-5805-bce3-ea016f3e92ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0305ffe-7471-5805-bce3-ea016f3e92ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2836c933-6423-5d47-b0d5-a225bde1662d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2729257-14f7-5d82-bd92-97d5a5b741d9" "answer-2836c933-6423-5d47-b0d5-a225bde1662d" "answer-fe01dfce-1c89-5728-a40a-4b936e3d1215" "answer-13f0ef2e-0f93-5e0a-8d71-92286357a7c7" "answer-da0e5662-6a8a-576b-b15e-de600a9ace05"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2729257-14f7-5d82-bd92-97d5a5b741d9" :answer/id #uuid "d2729257-14f7-5d82-bd92-97d5a5b741d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2836c933-6423-5d47-b0d5-a225bde1662d" :answer/id #uuid "2836c933-6423-5d47-b0d5-a225bde1662d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe01dfce-1c89-5728-a40a-4b936e3d1215" :answer/id #uuid "fe01dfce-1c89-5728-a40a-4b936e3d1215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13f0ef2e-0f93-5e0a-8d71-92286357a7c7" :answer/id #uuid "13f0ef2e-0f93-5e0a-8d71-92286357a7c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da0e5662-6a8a-576b-b15e-de600a9ace05" :answer/id #uuid "da0e5662-6a8a-576b-b15e-de600a9ace05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3b7c4644-c6ca-51e8-b34c-e72d6ef6b667" :question/id #uuid "3b7c4644-c6ca-51e8-b34c-e72d6ef6b667" :question/math-academy-id "q-161894" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi }^{u=2\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(-1+(-1))\\cdot (-(-1)+1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d5adb4bd-431e-53be-8fd6-9773762a903f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d5adb4bd-431e-53be-8fd6-9773762a903f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d5adb4bd-431e-53be-8fd6-9773762a903f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ab7b47f3-3802-5868-b5d4-8e9d5d18bbcd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1536ecd2-34e1-5711-8194-9090d29369f5" "answer-ab7b47f3-3802-5868-b5d4-8e9d5d18bbcd" "answer-e454e28a-b46a-5bb3-81f3-e70638e4c982" "answer-857fe175-09f7-5eb2-89ec-46c4061ca387" "answer-b3a944d1-2a3f-544a-a5da-c1c851119eb4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1536ecd2-34e1-5711-8194-9090d29369f5" :answer/id #uuid "1536ecd2-34e1-5711-8194-9090d29369f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab7b47f3-3802-5868-b5d4-8e9d5d18bbcd" :answer/id #uuid "ab7b47f3-3802-5868-b5d4-8e9d5d18bbcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e454e28a-b46a-5bb3-81f3-e70638e4c982" :answer/id #uuid "e454e28a-b46a-5bb3-81f3-e70638e4c982" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-857fe175-09f7-5eb2-89ec-46c4061ca387" :answer/id #uuid "857fe175-09f7-5eb2-89ec-46c4061ca387" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3a944d1-2a3f-544a-a5da-c1c851119eb4" :answer/id #uuid "b3a944d1-2a3f-544a-a5da-c1c851119eb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198964 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+z\\le 2,\\,-1\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,-1\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /6}^{u=\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186197654 :answer-field/correct 17592186197655 :answer-field/choices ["answer-828f75f9-fca6-5a07-93ba-87dbb17ec87e" "answer-55d05532-6ff9-54f2-9d4c-bf3ea90714ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-828f75f9-fca6-5a07-93ba-87dbb17ec87e" :answer/id #uuid "828f75f9-fca6-5a07-93ba-87dbb17ec87e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55d05532-6ff9-54f2-9d4c-bf3ea90714ff" :answer/id #uuid "55d05532-6ff9-54f2-9d4c-bf3ea90714ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198623 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3x-2y,\\,v=y+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(3x-2y)^{2}(y+2z)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le 3x-2y\\le 3,\\,0\\le y+2z\\le 2,\\,0\\le 2z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=3x-2y,\\,v=y+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(3x-2y)^{2}(y+2z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{2}v\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{2}v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{u}^{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1/2}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{u=0}^{u=3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (9-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186194369 :answer-field/correct 17592186194370 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b4b2886a-abaf-53c5-8dbb-583291c4a37e" :question/id #uuid "b4b2886a-abaf-53c5-8dbb-583291c4a37e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162211" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-694f0a99-bbd9-58dc-bc56-6c2fef66f7a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-694f0a99-bbd9-58dc-bc56-6c2fef66f7a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "694f0a99-bbd9-58dc-bc56-6c2fef66f7a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc7f0181-a616-56cf-9ee3-cd568924243c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1bb8c5ce-8dbf-5386-8235-8248286f77f5" "answer-0c637b6f-4f06-51ca-9616-b193dbfe0073" "answer-4ab67b62-04e2-59bc-825a-dfe5cf1d691d" "answer-547236d6-bb1e-5d75-97ea-1f9bb0063831" "answer-dc7f0181-a616-56cf-9ee3-cd568924243c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bb8c5ce-8dbf-5386-8235-8248286f77f5" :answer/id #uuid "1bb8c5ce-8dbf-5386-8235-8248286f77f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c637b6f-4f06-51ca-9616-b193dbfe0073" :answer/id #uuid "0c637b6f-4f06-51ca-9616-b193dbfe0073" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ab67b62-04e2-59bc-825a-dfe5cf1d691d" :answer/id #uuid "4ab67b62-04e2-59bc-825a-dfe5cf1d691d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-547236d6-bb1e-5d75-97ea-1f9bb0063831" :answer/id #uuid "547236d6-bb1e-5d75-97ea-1f9bb0063831" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc7f0181-a616-56cf-9ee3-cd568924243c" :answer/id #uuid "dc7f0181-a616-56cf-9ee3-cd568924243c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198965 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{y}+x\\le 2,\\,1\\le \\frac{1}{z+1}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=\\sqrt{y}+x,\\,w=\\frac{1}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -(z+1)^{-2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-(z+1)^{-2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(z+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={w}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}{w}^{-2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\cdot [-{w}^{-1}]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186197660 :answer-field/correct 17592186197663 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd1760e0-c4d7-5ae6-945d-3f3e267b54eb" :question/id #uuid "fd1760e0-c4d7-5ae6-945d-3f3e267b54eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162170" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}-x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le e\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}1\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=e} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-80cdcf0b-fbd2-5ef6-980d-ca6cc3566902"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-80cdcf0b-fbd2-5ef6-980d-ca6cc3566902" :answer-field/id #uuid "80cdcf0b-fbd2-5ef6-980d-ca6cc3566902" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-18ec478d-8c40-5efa-92a1-86ea6f027a90" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b962412-6d6a-5fe6-8c93-21ebb16264be" "answer-6c34f66d-0108-524b-a0bb-ea16f3872c5f" "answer-18ec478d-8c40-5efa-92a1-86ea6f027a90" "answer-24ed6cbd-b045-57ee-bdcf-da55c1ab6bff" "answer-b92bb4be-1ea9-57c7-8327-65aa672b6f3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b962412-6d6a-5fe6-8c93-21ebb16264be" :answer/id #uuid "6b962412-6d6a-5fe6-8c93-21ebb16264be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c34f66d-0108-524b-a0bb-ea16f3872c5f" :answer/id #uuid "6c34f66d-0108-524b-a0bb-ea16f3872c5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18ec478d-8c40-5efa-92a1-86ea6f027a90" :answer/id #uuid "18ec478d-8c40-5efa-92a1-86ea6f027a90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2(e-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24ed6cbd-b045-57ee-bdcf-da55c1ab6bff" :answer/id #uuid "24ed6cbd-b045-57ee-bdcf-da55c1ab6bff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b92bb4be-1ea9-57c7-8327-65aa672b6f3b" :answer/id #uuid "b92bb4be-1ea9-57c7-8327-65aa672b6f3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7144ca6a-debb-5e59-baf7-8232b8798b6c" :question/id #uuid "7144ca6a-debb-5e59-baf7-8232b8798b6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162183" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=\\frac{z-1}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-2\\le v\\le 0,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{z-1}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=u.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |u|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{5}u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{0}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}dw \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\cdot [v]_{v=-2}^{v=0}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (0-(-2))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6f66c5c-6087-5c29-8319-6c31eb512359"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6f66c5c-6087-5c29-8319-6c31eb512359" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f66c5c-6087-5c29-8319-6c31eb512359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0d41d4b8-c95d-5007-abd0-08600085f08f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0b43c30d-dbbd-56ae-bb9d-ec705a2a3b5c" "answer-0d41d4b8-c95d-5007-abd0-08600085f08f" "answer-11f2f0e2-ee85-5dc7-8bf8-e4bbb3c1a106" "answer-e6ad71c3-d535-5d86-9412-6ac3d6c65116" "answer-f3d483b0-65b2-53a0-9add-ad98d8a18577"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b43c30d-dbbd-56ae-bb9d-ec705a2a3b5c" :answer/id #uuid "0b43c30d-dbbd-56ae-bb9d-ec705a2a3b5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d41d4b8-c95d-5007-abd0-08600085f08f" :answer/id #uuid "0d41d4b8-c95d-5007-abd0-08600085f08f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11f2f0e2-ee85-5dc7-8bf8-e4bbb3c1a106" :answer/id #uuid "11f2f0e2-ee85-5dc7-8bf8-e4bbb3c1a106" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6ad71c3-d535-5d86-9412-6ac3d6c65116" :answer/id #uuid "e6ad71c3-d535-5d86-9412-6ac3d6c65116" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3d483b0-65b2-53a0-9add-ad98d8a18577" :answer/id #uuid "f3d483b0-65b2-53a0-9add-ad98d8a18577" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f69775da-3c2e-512a-8912-f342f7d0a00f" :question/id #uuid "f69775da-3c2e-512a-8912-f342f7d0a00f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162153" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+3z,\\,w={z}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3{z}^{2})-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{w=1}^{w=8} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot ({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1d2256b3-469f-5f0e-a313-ab48af9bfa87"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1d2256b3-469f-5f0e-a313-ab48af9bfa87" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d2256b3-469f-5f0e-a313-ab48af9bfa87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d1f28ea2-1428-5d1b-859c-d4b26aa341ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-42bc746b-b715-55f2-86ec-fd72bb4f4ef2" "answer-dd5075d2-9ee4-5c53-a21e-1de9889cd0a6" "answer-d1f28ea2-1428-5d1b-859c-d4b26aa341ef" "answer-fa7b48d0-b052-528a-ae87-b2b5f4f33257" "answer-4c8522ed-fdef-5b29-b95a-c55a7f972e48"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42bc746b-b715-55f2-86ec-fd72bb4f4ef2" :answer/id #uuid "42bc746b-b715-55f2-86ec-fd72bb4f4ef2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd5075d2-9ee4-5c53-a21e-1de9889cd0a6" :answer/id #uuid "dd5075d2-9ee4-5c53-a21e-1de9889cd0a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1f28ea2-1428-5d1b-859c-d4b26aa341ef" :answer/id #uuid "d1f28ea2-1428-5d1b-859c-d4b26aa341ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa7b48d0-b052-528a-ae87-b2b5f4f33257" :answer/id #uuid "fa7b48d0-b052-528a-ae87-b2b5f4f33257" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c8522ed-fdef-5b29-b95a-c55a7f972e48" :answer/id #uuid "4c8522ed-fdef-5b29-b95a-c55a7f972e48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199461 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: (u,v,w) \\longrightarrow \\left(x(u,v,w), \\, y(u,v,w), \\, z(u,v,w) \\right)\n$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^3,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = R,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d7/d7875753545cba8babd08fccaf55236c6b32bfc56f43f496343174acc198f1d9.png)\n\n \n\nTo compute the integral of a function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R,$ we use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for double integrals." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198490 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186161138 :answer-field/choices 17592186161139] [:db/retract 17592186161138 :answer-field/choices 17592186161140] [:db/retract 17592186161138 :answer-field/choices 17592186161141] [:db/retract 17592186161138 :answer-field/choices 17592186161142] [:db/retract 17592186161138 :answer-field/choices 17592186161143] [:db/retract 17592186161156 :answer-field/choices 17592186161157] [:db/retract 17592186161156 :answer-field/choices 17592186161158] [:db/retract 17592186161156 :answer-field/choices 17592186161159] [:db/retract 17592186161156 :answer-field/choices 17592186161160] [:db/retract 17592186161156 :answer-field/choices 17592186161161] {:db/id 17592186161997 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-26759ee4-9182-5088-bdc1-26b84fc02211" "question-cc24bfb9-272f-54fa-b546-5cdc700f6030" "question-07427b74-a9a0-5dbd-ab84-a95a81a836e0" "question-72db930f-dc67-5772-9a58-607de1ef27a6" "question-a12ce635-7077-5ae6-9899-fd940aea64f8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161789 :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/6c/6c54af85add7fd0b136f807ac3f65f84dabd948b21c16fcb4fb05fc41e3fab70.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq m) = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq m) = F(m) = 1-\\dfrac{1}{m^4} , \\quad m \\geq 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n1-\\dfrac{1}{m^4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad m = \\sqrt[4]2.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $m \\geq 1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161998 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2b515338-9a49-5088-8d83-3ec16988abce" "question-8d19c0b1-13a1-5100-8957-4c982f273723" "question-f4cab15e-7525-590b-aa74-c49be38d3cec" "question-a6d47759-e842-511b-a810-c9880823e33f" "question-714df0ea-5d53-53df-b0aa-14ba037b9bde"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161790 :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the probability density function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/e3/e3eb6147bfde4d3e4c65bad5054fc311cf561c1c869530bbf693f362a057033d.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq m) = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le m) & ={\\int }_{1}^{m}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}|}_{1}^{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}+1 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ m & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $m \\geq 1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161999 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a4e71952-c18f-5e5a-8bbb-dfd0f7ad678f" "question-dfe1ae13-0566-5996-84a0-b50d07468365" "question-7e41b2e9-6ace-571f-a3ef-6f43b41df0ec" "question-3ca6810e-a219-54cd-a8c8-f575cfccdc55"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161791 :question/problem "Find, to three decimal places, the interquartile range of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/be/be9a31bab2f359a49dc4c334a1584cee4d60b0f353e65c45a92409f928102daf.svg)" :question/worked-solution "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number $q_1$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq q_1) = \\dfrac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number $q_3$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq q_3) = \\dfrac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{q}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ {q}_{1} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{q}_{3}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3}^{2} & =\\frac{75}{4} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\nIQR = q_3 - q_1 =\\dfrac{5\\sqrt3}{2} - \\dfrac{5}{2}\\approx 1.830\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162000 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f4c7c35-e12b-53f3-8c46-ab140e4e9992" "question-537b608f-d718-5a66-8b0b-7dabfac0375b" "question-36945eec-4c74-5f74-8f42-dd9a1f9a922a" "question-049efa61-2f95-573c-8d4c-3a15cf157276"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161792 :question/problem "Find the $27$th percentile of the random variable $X$ if the probability distribution function of $X$ is given by\n![](images/4e/4ea6c51080e39f5ea4d293d0265c0b5ddf5001f638bd4177646f29e2140bf279.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The $100p$th percentile of a random variable $X$ is the number $x_p$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq x_p) = p.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we want to find the $27$th percentile, which corresponds to the decimal $p=0.27.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {x}_{0.27}) & ={\\int }_{5}^{{x}_{0.27}}\\frac{2}{75}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{75}|}_{5}^{{x}_{0.27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{0.27}^{2}}{75}-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $27$th percentile is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{0.27}^{2}}{75}-\\frac{1}{3} & =0.27 \\\\ {x}_{0.27}^{2} & =75(\\frac{27}{100}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {x}_{0.27} & =6.727\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-26759ee4-9182-5088-bdc1-26b84fc02211" :question/id #uuid "26759ee4-9182-5088-bdc1-26b84fc02211" :question/math-academy-id "q-125510" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{3}}{64},\\,0\\le m\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{3}}{64}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\sqrt[3]{32}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a8c632b-7961-5fad-8319-cad8210fb3f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a8c632b-7961-5fad-8319-cad8210fb3f5" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8c632b-7961-5fad-8319-cad8210fb3f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4b1dae7a-d1c2-5268-ab76-bb5800e65796" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d675510a-f4a2-5e75-8380-eb715fc51139" "answer-1138d2b7-a3e3-5532-97b5-c5fc9d0129c6" "answer-a48a09bd-cecc-5482-94cb-5f60175e9e4e" "answer-4b1dae7a-d1c2-5268-ab76-bb5800e65796" "answer-62c8e9a7-f26a-5628-88e2-f271c7a23633"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d675510a-f4a2-5e75-8380-eb715fc51139" :answer/id #uuid "d675510a-f4a2-5e75-8380-eb715fc51139" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1138d2b7-a3e3-5532-97b5-c5fc9d0129c6" :answer/id #uuid "1138d2b7-a3e3-5532-97b5-c5fc9d0129c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a48a09bd-cecc-5482-94cb-5f60175e9e4e" :answer/id #uuid "a48a09bd-cecc-5482-94cb-5f60175e9e4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{32}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b1dae7a-d1c2-5268-ab76-bb5800e65796" :answer/id #uuid "4b1dae7a-d1c2-5268-ab76-bb5800e65796" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62c8e9a7-f26a-5628-88e2-f271c7a23633" :answer/id #uuid "62c8e9a7-f26a-5628-88e2-f271c7a23633" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cc24bfb9-272f-54fa-b546-5cdc700f6030" :question/id #uuid "cc24bfb9-272f-54fa-b546-5cdc700f6030" :question/math-academy-id "q-94598" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}, & 0\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25},\\,0\\le m\\le 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{m}^{2}}{25}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $0\\le m\\le 5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4273a9c4-b2af-5b72-ac14-00e304bf07b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4273a9c4-b2af-5b72-ac14-00e304bf07b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "4273a9c4-b2af-5b72-ac14-00e304bf07b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f9dbcca-797e-55ba-8abc-78fc021a5214" :answer-field/choices ["answer-64c196ad-4c8a-588e-b2ff-6bdd2ba18aae" "answer-bb7b0abc-c581-56fb-bb65-2a52485c0bd4" "answer-bc448948-1a97-5631-ba43-c323b32dee1c" "answer-5012aee4-8029-59e9-a001-1983600f8879" "answer-6f9dbcca-797e-55ba-8abc-78fc021a5214"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64c196ad-4c8a-588e-b2ff-6bdd2ba18aae" :answer/id #uuid "64c196ad-4c8a-588e-b2ff-6bdd2ba18aae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb7b0abc-c581-56fb-bb65-2a52485c0bd4" :answer/id #uuid "bb7b0abc-c581-56fb-bb65-2a52485c0bd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc448948-1a97-5631-ba43-c323b32dee1c" :answer/id #uuid "bc448948-1a97-5631-ba43-c323b32dee1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5012aee4-8029-59e9-a001-1983600f8879" :answer/id #uuid "5012aee4-8029-59e9-a001-1983600f8879" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f9dbcca-797e-55ba-8abc-78fc021a5214" :answer/id #uuid "6f9dbcca-797e-55ba-8abc-78fc021a5214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-07427b74-a9a0-5dbd-ab84-a95a81a836e0" :question/id #uuid "07427b74-a9a0-5dbd-ab84-a95a81a836e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125516" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{195}+\\frac{38}{65}, & 2<x\\le 3, \\\\ 1, & x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $F(2)=\\frac{2}{3}>\\frac{1}{2},$ we have $0\\le m\\le 2.$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{m}{3}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-37430a3d-0d71-5551-a19e-aa1fdf2b1819"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-37430a3d-0d71-5551-a19e-aa1fdf2b1819" :answer-field/id #uuid "37430a3d-0d71-5551-a19e-aa1fdf2b1819" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3050f898-36aa-5645-a5a4-af8b4ba8fde3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca571706-b58d-5784-b937-566421c78a8b" "answer-f88d6025-6a3c-5c70-abf4-3698342d3349" "answer-92a7b198-9238-51a4-b8ab-d4d1a87d50ac" "answer-e38ccb96-44ea-55c6-99c9-dae1e3a87ad7" "answer-3050f898-36aa-5645-a5a4-af8b4ba8fde3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca571706-b58d-5784-b937-566421c78a8b" :answer/id #uuid "ca571706-b58d-5784-b937-566421c78a8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f88d6025-6a3c-5c70-abf4-3698342d3349" :answer/id #uuid "f88d6025-6a3c-5c70-abf4-3698342d3349" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92a7b198-9238-51a4-b8ab-d4d1a87d50ac" :answer/id #uuid "92a7b198-9238-51a4-b8ab-d4d1a87d50ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e38ccb96-44ea-55c6-99c9-dae1e3a87ad7" :answer/id #uuid "e38ccb96-44ea-55c6-99c9-dae1e3a87ad7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3050f898-36aa-5645-a5a4-af8b4ba8fde3" :answer/id #uuid "3050f898-36aa-5645-a5a4-af8b4ba8fde3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-72db930f-dc67-5772-9a58-607de1ef27a6" :question/id #uuid "72db930f-dc67-5772-9a58-607de1ef27a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-125515" :question/problem "Find the median of the random variable $X$ if the cumulative distribution function of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2, \\\\ \\frac{1}{240}({x}^{4}-16), & 2\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The median of a random variable $X$ is the number $m$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\le m)=F(m)=\\frac{1}{240}({m}^{4}-16),\\,2\\le m\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the median is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{240}({m}^{4}-16)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,m=\\sqrt[4]{136}.\n$$\n\nNote that we took the positive root because $2\\le m\\le 4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa5b07f4-7bd6-5b7b-b298-c1c8eac0c1bd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa5b07f4-7bd6-5b7b-b298-c1c8eac0c1bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5b07f4-7bd6-5b7b-b298-c1c8eac0c1bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-74401d18-1e97-5151-b206-bf19cb24f8a1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0bfcbbaa-a8cc-51e4-be1a-c7f73905315a" "answer-9543e404-9adb-5088-a2f1-e2be2229a985" "answer-f028fb9b-aaf2-5ba7-81b1-86734cbf6171" "answer-e4851740-2d3d-54dc-b48c-3c099aec6c2e" "answer-74401d18-1e97-5151-b206-bf19cb24f8a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer