86131479 :answer-field/choices 17592186131484] {:db/id 17592186134240 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we obtained ${\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=x.$ This simply means that $x$ lies in the plane, i.e., \n\n$$\nx\\in \\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186131479 :answer-field/correct "answer-f9ebe8cb-1296-59b7-a623-8598530dfc06" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5d54a5dc-dba6-59f8-ae60-19787c66f64c" "answer-17fc5669-e1a5-503b-aa6a-8980f4bfb59c" "answer-f9ebe8cb-1296-59b7-a623-8598530dfc06" "answer-77fe3304-012d-58d4-ae13-7ee1364c0319" "answer-4a78b053-5581-56fc-a00e-472352b4bfc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d54a5dc-dba6-59f8-ae60-19787c66f64c" :answer/id #uuid "5d54a5dc-dba6-59f8-ae60-19787c66f64c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17fc5669-e1a5-503b-aa6a-8980f4bfb59c" :answer/id #uuid "17fc5669-e1a5-503b-aa6a-8980f4bfb59c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9ebe8cb-1296-59b7-a623-8598530dfc06" :answer/id #uuid "f9ebe8cb-1296-59b7-a623-8598530dfc06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77fe3304-012d-58d4-ae13-7ee1364c0319" :answer/id #uuid "77fe3304-012d-58d4-ae13-7ee1364c0319" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a78b053-5581-56fc-a00e-472352b4bfc2" :answer/id #uuid "4a78b053-5581-56fc-a00e-472352b4bfc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326855 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186381153 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that we obtained ${\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=x.$ This simply means that $x$ lies in the plane, i.e., \n\n$$\nx\\in \\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186389002 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186394518 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186388723 :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186382580 :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186275452 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}34 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{38}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 34\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134296 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272103 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135049 :tutorial/content "We've already seen how to find the orthogonal projection of a vector onto a subspace in the case when the subspace is defined using an orthogonal basis. In this lesson, we'll learn how to do the same thing in the case of non-orthogonal bases.\n\nFor example, consider the following vectors:\n![](images/28/28ce7470231775d8889555d0dd9f37ee9215044762a40335bd43578b121486a3.svg)\nHow can we find the orthogonal projection $\\mathbf{x}_S$ of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}?$ Notice that since $\\mathbf a_1\\cdot \\mathbf a_2 \\neq 0,$ the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are not orthogonal.\n\nAs usual, we would like to write $\\mathbf{x}$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\in S$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\!\\in S^\\perp.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a5576fcaf9e0be6fc14978eb91dcbaceee75cbfddaa537fdb05d34b8e31e2e0.png)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp,$ that is, $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\perp S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}⊥{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the expression for $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ into the system to get\n![](images/f1/f1e3c7ae9e966ebcab88901c9e44a5449cb98716f43fc526d306e0af5186923b.svg)\n\nWe can write this system in an equivalent matrix form as follows:\n![](images/47/472c41e1e509d2db9c41dfa95127382eeba7ab5fbaa4172d680c39335694ef2e.svg)\n\nIf we denote \n![](images/26/2650c1020d413a7c988279cc698ab4b7c4caff427615d49b35ef4e9a42651ff0.svg)\nand ![](images/9b/9b50884bd0af404e71f9d7734e9bc054fc9439aba02780cf2beb5fa0a6ac1f32.svg) then we obtain\n\n$$\nA^TA\\mathbf{k} = A^T\\mathbf{x}\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{k}=(A^TA)^{-1}A^T\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nAlso, we note that  $\\mathbf{x}_S = k_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + k_2 \\mathbf{a}_2$ can be expressed as $\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x}\t= A\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =Ak \\\\  & =A({A}^{T}A)^{-1}{A}^{T}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a formula for the orthogonal projection!\n\nNow, we return to our example. Using the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 11\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note**: Pay attention to the order in which we carried out the multiplication. Carrying out the operations in the order shown above can save us a lot of work!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-eaaccc28-17cb-56b1-b96e-6187ebe91480"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-eaaccc28-17cb-56b1-b96e-6187ebe91480" :question/id #uuid "eaaccc28-17cb-56b1-b96e-6187ebe91480" :question/math-academy-id "q-130203" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}=1-{x}^{2}+2y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =1-{x}^{2}+2y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)j \\\\  & =(2y-2)i-2x\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2)i-2(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+2\\,j.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ad3bafa-b0de-589d-9a11-64d4563a5603"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ad3bafa-b0de-589d-9a11-64d4563a5603" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad3bafa-b0de-589d-9a11-64d4563a5603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8054282c-e26d-5511-a066-a463f3020bfe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-73996ca2-8d53-576d-9599-e7d064a54b16" "answer-b5877c60-a794-5905-b476-5b03a5663061" "answer-f6a04f0f-fb4c-5c47-a171-0ccaf2eedb86" "answer-8054282c-e26d-5511-a066-a463f3020bfe" "answer-644861a7-1cbc-5867-8185-defe9888db93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73996ca2-8d53-576d-9599-e7d064a54b16" :answer/id #uuid "73996ca2-8d53-576d-9599-e7d064a54b16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5877c60-a794-5905-b476-5b03a5663061" :answer/id #uuid "b5877c60-a794-5905-b476-5b03a5663061" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-i" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6a04f0f-fb4c-5c47-a171-0ccaf2eedb86" :answer/id #uuid "f6a04f0f-fb4c-5c47-a171-0ccaf2eedb86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-i+2\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8054282c-e26d-5511-a066-a463f3020bfe" :answer/id #uuid "8054282c-e26d-5511-a066-a463f3020bfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-644861a7-1cbc-5867-8185-defe9888db93" :answer/id #uuid "644861a7-1cbc-5867-8185-defe9888db93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-12b9dfa6-9b29-5bbc-991a-88ee2275afa9" :activity/id #uuid "12b9dfa6-9b29-5bbc-991a-88ee2275afa9" :activity/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049511 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-05e7d7d3-d815-5b26-b630-27644d17a707" "step-8010488e-cbf0-5349-af76-b70ec53c1a3b" "step-6e92b42c-037c-5e2c-9313-5354ee48f332" "step-02f310c0-3a63-5ca4-a009-b9bb2d7c2097" "step-d3f5afa9-3e2d-58c5-8c55-a63599bc185e"] :activity/first-step "step-05e7d7d3-d815-5b26-b630-27644d17a707" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-05e7d7d3-d815-5b26-b630-27644d17a707" :step/id #uuid "05e7d7d3-d815-5b26-b630-27644d17a707" :step/content 17592186323918 :step/next "step-8010488e-cbf0-5349-af76-b70ec53c1a3b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8010488e-cbf0-5349-af76-b70ec53c1a3b" :step/id #uuid "8010488e-cbf0-5349-af76-b70ec53c1a3b" :step/content 17592186339110 :step/next "step-6e92b42c-037c-5e2c-9313-5354ee48f332" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6e92b42c-037c-5e2c-9313-5354ee48f332" :step/id #uuid "6e92b42c-037c-5e2c-9313-5354ee48f332" :step/content 17592186403571 :step/next "step-02f310c0-3a63-5ca4-a009-b9bb2d7c2097" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-02f310c0-3a63-5ca4-a009-b9bb2d7c2097" :step/id #uuid "02f310c0-3a63-5ca4-a009-b9bb2d7c2097" :step/content "question-eaaccc28-17cb-56b1-b96e-6187ebe91480" :step/next "step-d3f5afa9-3e2d-58c5-8c55-a63599bc185e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d3f5afa9-3e2d-58c5-8c55-a63599bc185e" :step/id #uuid "d3f5afa9-3e2d-58c5-8c55-a63599bc185e" :step/content 17592186373757 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "activity-faea1c30-e59b-555e-986e-d9c6a2962d7e" :activity/id #uuid "faea1c30-e59b-555e-986e-d9c6a2962d7e" :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050687 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-6f10ce92-f069-526b-924f-aad5da92df4d" "step-664817ed-1c5d-56c6-9317-1db891f36e84" "step-c50e5f01-04d4-55ae-95c5-9580fdda17a4" "step-e608e1df-6587-55b1-9e73-f808cc254e61" "step-d68c1073-788b-5de9-b32e-40a492159af1"] :activity/first-step "step-6f10ce92-f069-526b-924f-aad5da92df4d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-6f10ce92-f069-526b-924f-aad5da92df4d" :step/id #uuid "6f10ce92-f069-526b-924f-aad5da92df4d" :step/content 17592186259609 :step/next "step-664817ed-1c5d-56c6-9317-1db891f36e84" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-664817ed-1c5d-56c6-9317-1db891f36e84" :step/id #uuid "664817ed-1c5d-56c6-9317-1db891f36e84" :step/content 17592186259610 :step/next "step-c50e5f01-04d4-55ae-95c5-9580fdda17a4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c50e5f01-04d4-55ae-95c5-9580fdda17a4" :step/id #uuid "c50e5f01-04d4-55ae-95c5-9580fdda17a4" :step/content 17592186415680 :step/next "step-e608e1df-6587-55b1-9e73-f808cc254e61" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e608e1df-6587-55b1-9e73-f808cc254e61" :step/id #uuid "e608e1df-6587-55b1-9e73-f808cc254e61" :step/content 17592186259670 :step/next "step-d68c1073-788b-5de9-b32e-40a492159af1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d68c1073-788b-5de9-b32e-40a492159af1" :step/id #uuid "d68c1073-788b-5de9-b32e-40a492159af1" :step/content 17592186259671 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3c788761-8a3d-5a84-9fc6-a55521c1f3bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3c788761-8a3d-5a84-9fc6-a55521c1f3bb" :question/id #uuid "3c788761-8a3d-5a84-9fc6-a55521c1f3bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109579" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a676cad1-550b-58ad-bc2d-4c3f63df1e8e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a676cad1-550b-58ad-bc2d-4c3f63df1e8e" :answer-field/id #uuid "a676cad1-550b-58ad-bc2d-4c3f63df1e8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-deb67241-8ca5-5879-a251-c812cf6ac763" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3266316f-36b1-5ff6-a6b0-47a3434780c2" "answer-4ca34fc0-97a3-5fa6-97d2-26038deb9c24" "answer-08db4f16-a7e9-5314-b935-a3eb368dfcef" "answer-deb67241-8ca5-5879-a251-c812cf6ac763" "answer-e80baa43-8f8f-5491-84c0-0c880c87ed98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3266316f-36b1-5ff6-a6b0-47a3434780c2" :answer/id #uuid "3266316f-36b1-5ff6-a6b0-47a3434780c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ca34fc0-97a3-5fa6-97d2-26038deb9c24" :answer/id #uuid "4ca34fc0-97a3-5fa6-97d2-26038deb9c24" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08db4f16-a7e9-5314-b935-a3eb368dfcef" :answer/id #uuid "08db4f16-a7e9-5314-b935-a3eb368dfcef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-deb67241-8ca5-5879-a251-c812cf6ac763" :answer/id #uuid "deb67241-8ca5-5879-a251-c812cf6ac763" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e80baa43-8f8f-5491-84c0-0c880c87ed98" :answer/id #uuid "e80baa43-8f8f-5491-84c0-0c880c87ed98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-65743f6d-c4f5-542f-9721-c15aa32b036f" :activity/id #uuid "65743f6d-c4f5-542f-9721-c15aa32b036f" :activity/title "Generalized Eigenvectors" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049408 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-be1b9ef5-0697-50a6-856c-790402f18621" "step-50add218-dc98-586d-b53f-7221b90a299b" "step-7ae106f8-dc27-52d1-9ba2-63f8ff076871" "step-378512a5-a4a3-5cfe-ac0c-8e6b41f621a9" "step-85151b46-52f9-5130-a26f-ee239d7c5fc8"] :activity/first-step "step-be1b9ef5-0697-50a6-856c-790402f18621" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-be1b9ef5-0697-50a6-856c-790402f18621" :step/id #uuid "be1b9ef5-0697-50a6-856c-790402f18621" :step/content 17592186244291 :step/next "step-50add218-dc98-586d-b53f-7221b90a299b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-50add218-dc98-586d-b53f-7221b90a299b" :step/id #uuid "50add218-dc98-586d-b53f-7221b90a299b" :step/content 17592186413000 :step/next "step-7ae106f8-dc27-52d1-9ba2-63f8ff076871" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7ae106f8-dc27-52d1-9ba2-63f8ff076871" :step/id #uuid "7ae106f8-dc27-52d1-9ba2-63f8ff076871" :step/content 17592186247001 :step/next "step-378512a5-a4a3-5cfe-ac0c-8e6b41f621a9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-378512a5-a4a3-5cfe-ac0c-8e6b41f621a9" :step/id #uuid "378512a5-a4a3-5cfe-ac0c-8e6b41f621a9" :step/content "question-3c788761-8a3d-5a84-9fc6-a55521c1f3bb" :step/next "step-85151b46-52f9-5130-a26f-ee239d7c5fc8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-85151b46-52f9-5130-a26f-ee239d7c5fc8" :step/id #uuid "85151b46-52f9-5130-a26f-ee239d7c5fc8" :step/content 17592186247012 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186228298 :answer-field/choices 17592186228299] [:db/retract 17592186228298 :answer-field/choices 17592186228300] [:db/retract 17592186228298 :answer-field/choices 17592186228301] [:db/retract 17592186228298 :answer-field/choices 17592186228302] [:db/retract 17592186228298 :answer-field/choices 17592186228303] {:db/id 17592186229143 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d66735c3-ba98-5f1c-8e1d-7d80133c59cf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d66735c3-ba98-5f1c-8e1d-7d80133c59cf" :question/id #uuid "d66735c3-ba98-5f1c-8e1d-7d80133c59cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-335266" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bf4268c8-502e-5529-b80f-13d26b351b38"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bf4268c8-502e-5529-b80f-13d26b351b38" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf4268c8-502e-5529-b80f-13d26b351b38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bcf2cf7c-d4c9-58ad-ac1c-f6f73d55b4fe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c9df7eb-dd1e-53e0-a87d-68b6fd1f480c" "answer-c6299321-746c-562a-a610-1a2b40cd8433" "answer-bcf2cf7c-d4c9-58ad-ac1c-f6f73d55b4fe" "answer-b3959273-da78-54bd-9652-5e91ff910d6e" "answer-ddfaca09-e572-55e6-9c7f-bc94a914bffa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c9df7eb-dd1e-53e0-a87d-68b6fd1f480c" :answer/id #uuid "0c9df7eb-dd1e-53e0-a87d-68b6fd1f480c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6299321-746c-562a-a610-1a2b40cd8433" :answer/id #uuid "c6299321-746c-562a-a610-1a2b40cd8433" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcf2cf7c-d4c9-58ad-ac1c-f6f73d55b4fe" :answer/id #uuid "bcf2cf7c-d4c9-58ad-ac1c-f6f73d55b4fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3959273-da78-54bd-9652-5e91ff910d6e" :answer/id #uuid "b3959273-da78-54bd-9652-5e91ff910d6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddfaca09-e572-55e6-9c7f-bc94a914bffa" :answer/id #uuid "ddfaca09-e572-55e6-9c7f-bc94a914bffa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229144 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b5cac624-fcaa-5546-985b-94ad2df95205"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b5cac624-fcaa-5546-985b-94ad2df95205" :question/id #uuid "b5cac624-fcaa-5546-985b-94ad2df95205" :question/math-academy-id "q-335436" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(3) \\\\ y(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "% Explanation\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-3)}\\,x(3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-199ebf62-577b-5ccf-9a87-eb53b5ca9e94" "answer-field-1cf580ea-9ff4-59fc-a20f-31f6a8ae4bf6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-199ebf62-577b-5ccf-9a87-eb53b5ca9e94" :answer-field/id #uuid "199ebf62-577b-5ccf-9a87-eb53b5ca9e94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e0f7256d-2d29-5c85-9d4b-72a842efcbc5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e0f7256d-2d29-5c85-9d4b-72a842efcbc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0f7256d-2d29-5c85-9d4b-72a842efcbc5" :answer/id #uuid "e0f7256d-2d29-5c85-9d4b-72a842efcbc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\cos(6(t-3))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1cf580ea-9ff4-59fc-a20f-31f6a8ae4bf6" :answer-field/id #uuid "1cf580ea-9ff4-59fc-a20f-31f6a8ae4bf6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c1cf21ed-005b-59e4-9bd4-7e59ab89d886" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c1cf21ed-005b-59e4-9bd4-7e59ab89d886"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1cf21ed-005b-59e4-9bd4-7e59ab89d886" :answer/id #uuid "c1cf21ed-005b-59e4-9bd4-7e59ab89d886" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sin(6(t-3))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229684 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(3) \\\\ y(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-3)}\\,x(3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228298 :answer-field/correct "answer-d9c4b7e3-8500-59d1-966d-52216ee1c994" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d9c4b7e3-8500-59d1-966d-52216ee1c994" "answer-d4207d80-a6eb-5165-8ab9-aace8ee2daa6" "answer-59d8cb4f-ee06-5141-9f71-0005f275d41d" "answer-65ee1f50-cf6b-58b8-afd8-663783704016" "answer-7e285e86-01d4-53c2-bae8-84c094816e4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9c4b7e3-8500-59d1-966d-52216ee1c994" :answer/id #uuid "d9c4b7e3-8500-59d1-966d-52216ee1c994" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4207d80-a6eb-5165-8ab9-aace8ee2daa6" :answer/id #uuid "d4207d80-a6eb-5165-8ab9-aace8ee2daa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59d8cb4f-ee06-5141-9f71-0005f275d41d" :answer/id #uuid "59d8cb4f-ee06-5141-9f71-0005f275d41d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65ee1f50-cf6b-58b8-afd8-663783704016" :answer/id #uuid "65ee1f50-cf6b-58b8-afd8-663783704016" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e285e86-01d4-53c2-bae8-84c094816e4b" :answer/id #uuid "7e285e86-01d4-53c2-bae8-84c094816e4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229145 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1163c64f-8eb6-5387-bd44-2f1d21a98032" "question-f598b188-bddf-5e21-881f-0c355a5c9889"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1163c64f-8eb6-5387-bd44-2f1d21a98032" :question/id #uuid "1163c64f-8eb6-5387-bd44-2f1d21a98032" :question/math-academy-id "q-336479" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by  \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, every solution can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is defined as the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nThe second condition, ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},$ is exactly line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by $\\text{using the initial condition }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L4}$ we know ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in the equation $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L5}.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{replacing }{Φ}_{0}(0)\\text{ with }{I}_{n}\\text{ in }x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2c1a9e2c-69c2-57dc-98a4-cfafabe3fc1e" "answer-field-31d8bd8e-b3aa-594c-88a8-b125c8eb1696" "answer-field-d6570167-3f96-5d88-b259-3613309f60d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2c1a9e2c-69c2-57dc-98a4-cfafabe3fc1e" :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1a9e2c-69c2-57dc-98a4-cfafabe3fc1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-09fc895b-be35-574c-a0f1-d2e9dbdc5824" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4b37ee0-60b6-54bb-88d3-5a9b8ced5669" "answer-96028a64-5f62-51b8-b0ec-6376058e920e" "answer-09fc895b-be35-574c-a0f1-d2e9dbdc5824" "answer-0ed34351-e7a9-51f4-906b-adec1b5a834b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4b37ee0-60b6-54bb-88d3-5a9b8ced5669" :answer/id #uuid "d4b37ee0-60b6-54bb-88d3-5a9b8ced5669" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "definition of a solution curve" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96028a64-5f62-51b8-b0ec-6376058e920e" :answer/id #uuid "96028a64-5f62-51b8-b0ec-6376058e920e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09fc895b-be35-574c-a0f1-d2e9dbdc5824" :answer/id #uuid "09fc895b-be35-574c-a0f1-d2e9dbdc5824" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "properties of a fundamental matrix" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ed34351-e7a9-51f4-906b-adec1b5a834b" :answer/id #uuid "0ed34351-e7a9-51f4-906b-adec1b5a834b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "properties of the determinant" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-31d8bd8e-b3aa-594c-88a8-b125c8eb1696" :answer-field/id #uuid "31d8bd8e-b3aa-594c-88a8-b125c8eb1696" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8b33370f-a59f-59ec-a300-6d743e8570be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9aa91ff-5e69-576a-ba8f-43568cbef52e" "answer-7155f794-f353-5a0c-8911-a1f285b25e19" "answer-820a69e5-51c6-5ddf-9a50-13e477a4befa" "answer-8b33370f-a59f-59ec-a300-6d743e8570be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9aa91ff-5e69-576a-ba8f-43568cbef52e" :answer/id #uuid "e9aa91ff-5e69-576a-ba8f-43568cbef52e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "applying the chain rule to ${Φ}_{0}(t)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7155f794-f353-5a0c-8911-a1f285b25e19" :answer/id #uuid "7155f794-f353-5a0c-8911-a1f285b25e19" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "computing $det({Φ}_{0}(0))$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-820a69e5-51c6-5ddf-9a50-13e477a4befa" :answer/id #uuid "820a69e5-51c6-5ddf-9a50-13e477a4befa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "differentiating at $t=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b33370f-a59f-59ec-a300-6d743e8570be" :answer/id #uuid "8b33370f-a59f-59ec-a300-6d743e8570be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "using the initial condition ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6570167-3f96-5d88-b259-3613309f60d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6570167-3f96-5d88-b259-3613309f60d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-11948125-54a4-5bb6-9128-a9f6fecd3d5a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4e2076e-2a67-59cb-8006-dae1d2be288f" "answer-e4b3c268-1dd4-5950-ab2e-09770223774b" "answer-e4f930bb-5307-59dd-80a7-b9bdb0cade2e" "answer-11948125-54a4-5bb6-9128-a9f6fecd3d5a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4e2076e-2a67-59cb-8006-dae1d2be288f" :answer/id #uuid "e4e2076e-2a67-59cb-8006-dae1d2be288f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "applying the chain rule" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4b3c268-1dd4-5950-ab2e-09770223774b" :answer/id #uuid "e4b3c268-1dd4-5950-ab2e-09770223774b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4f930bb-5307-59dd-80a7-b9bdb0cade2e" :answer/id #uuid "e4f930bb-5307-59dd-80a7-b9bdb0cade2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "integrating $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11948125-54a4-5bb6-9128-a9f6fecd3d5a" :answer/id #uuid "11948125-54a4-5bb6-9128-a9f6fecd3d5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f598b188-bddf-5e21-881f-0c355a5c9889" :question/id #uuid "f598b188-bddf-5e21-881f-0c355a5c9889" :question/math-academy-id "q-337119" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L6}$, and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\text{L6}$, we substitute $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can combine the exponentials:\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7}$ gives line $\\text{L8}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by $\\text{the fact that }{e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9f300979-2c03-5bca-8541-1101107fdf73" "answer-field-b775a29d-3fe5-5512-9ef6-783e154724b1" "answer-field-3ad0248d-955a-5ee8-87b5-a6a9266226fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9f300979-2c03-5bca-8541-1101107fdf73" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f300979-2c03-5bca-8541-1101107fdf73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-3ba11d7e-2a61-553a-9f1e-d3dc6f191266" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ba11d7e-2a61-553a-9f1e-d3dc6f191266" "answer-887d453d-74c3-5783-9f8d-da874648d69f" "answer-9bd63aad-1f80-570f-9351-385e1f17ff12"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ba11d7e-2a61-553a-9f1e-d3dc6f191266" :answer/id #uuid "3ba11d7e-2a61-553a-9f1e-d3dc6f191266" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-887d453d-74c3-5783-9f8d-da874648d69f" :answer/id #uuid "887d453d-74c3-5783-9f8d-da874648d69f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}=I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bd63aad-1f80-570f-9351-385e1f17ff12" :answer/id #uuid "9bd63aad-1f80-570f-9351-385e1f17ff12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{A{t}_{0}}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}=-I" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b775a29d-3fe5-5512-9ef6-783e154724b1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b775a29d-3fe5-5512-9ef6-783e154724b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-1edc9f2b-095b-537f-8b55-2d74f8563acc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16aae11e-7722-5d5d-a3ff-56d6a8a48158" "answer-619ca3f7-48eb-583d-ab51-495c74c6bef3" "answer-1edc9f2b-095b-537f-8b55-2d74f8563acc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16aae11e-7722-5d5d-a3ff-56d6a8a48158" :answer/id #uuid "16aae11e-7722-5d5d-a3ff-56d6a8a48158" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "evaluating line $\\text{L3}$ at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-619ca3f7-48eb-583d-ab51-495c74c6bef3" :answer/id #uuid "619ca3f7-48eb-583d-ab51-495c74c6bef3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "multiplying line $\\text{L3}$ on the right by ${e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1edc9f2b-095b-537f-8b55-2d74f8563acc" :answer/id #uuid "1edc9f2b-095b-537f-8b55-2d74f8563acc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "substituting $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ into $x(t)={e}^{At}c$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3ad0248d-955a-5ee8-87b5-a6a9266226fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad0248d-955a-5ee8-87b5-a6a9266226fa" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-aecba564-d67f-5fda-a785-7112b2e9e6f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61796693-027e-5e34-ac41-7245117c63ad" "answer-aecba564-d67f-5fda-a785-7112b2e9e6f1" "answer-67302da8-f87a-5742-b68d-b6432fc52247"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61796693-027e-5e34-ac41-7245117c63ad" :answer/id #uuid "61796693-027e-5e34-ac41-7245117c63ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the definition of the matrix exponential" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aecba564-d67f-5fda-a785-7112b2e9e6f1" :answer/id #uuid "aecba564-d67f-5fda-a785-7112b2e9e6f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67302da8-f87a-5742-b68d-b6432fc52247" :answer/id #uuid "67302da8-f87a-5742-b68d-b6432fc52247" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the identity ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{XY}$ for matrices" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-14357ccb-7387-5d90-95d5-9ec0c77cd805" :activity/id #uuid "14357ccb-7387-5d90-95d5-9ec0c77cd805" :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048231 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-2379b8ac-5990-5a88-876d-7a3b13ce4148" "step-b0d055da-4dd2-51f2-ae55-dae45284bf27" "step-c1447d08-16ad-539a-8ebf-f17fc52a738b" "step-28f5bdee-7eed-595c-9820-abd9f0486f9a" "step-7f8b56f9-e657-5fae-8f23-51af234be7a5"] :activity/first-step "step-2379b8ac-5990-5a88-876d-7a3b13ce4148" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-2379b8ac-5990-5a88-876d-7a3b13ce4148" :step/id #uuid "2379b8ac-5990-5a88-876d-7a3b13ce4148" :step/content "question-d66735c3-ba98-5f1c-8e1d-7d80133c59cf" :step/next "step-b0d055da-4dd2-51f2-ae55-dae45284bf27" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b0d055da-4dd2-51f2-ae55-dae45284bf27" :step/id #uuid "b0d055da-4dd2-51f2-ae55-dae45284bf27" :step/content "question-b5cac624-fcaa-5546-985b-94ad2df95205" :step/next "step-c1447d08-16ad-539a-8ebf-f17fc52a738b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c1447d08-16ad-539a-8ebf-f17fc52a738b" :step/id #uuid "c1447d08-16ad-539a-8ebf-f17fc52a738b" :step/content 17592186229684 :step/next "step-28f5bdee-7eed-595c-9820-abd9f0486f9a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-28f5bdee-7eed-595c-9820-abd9f0486f9a" :step/id #uuid "28f5bdee-7eed-595c-9820-abd9f0486f9a" :step/content "question-1163c64f-8eb6-5387-bd44-2f1d21a98032" :step/next "step-7f8b56f9-e657-5fae-8f23-51af234be7a5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7f8b56f9-e657-5fae-8f23-51af234be7a5" :step/id #uuid "7f8b56f9-e657-5fae-8f23-51af234be7a5" :step/content "question-f598b188-bddf-5e21-881f-0c355a5c9889" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133834 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-61556a8f-0a92-5ecd-8c20-5948347f1756"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351293 :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/73/7316d667d7308ac5ec0f5348055d3d0e7eb90b97abaa74f5693c3151dc3b8bb1.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-{x}^{2} & =x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/aa/aab6fb99694b518bfa9d200a75876c9e888ba1ab7d699464bc9caca360be775a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,x+1\\le y\\le 3-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351295 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(64-24)-(1-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351369 :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[y]_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-61556a8f-0a92-5ecd-8c20-5948347f1756" :question/id #uuid "61556a8f-0a92-5ecd-8c20-5948347f1756" :question/math-academy-id "q-132221" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a68077b18f8c08fc7cd68889b6472373c7677bcfcb6f370abce1e93cf67c4594.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line $x=0,$ and the curves $y=5-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}x & =5-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(2x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard the solution $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ since $x\\le 0$ in our region. Therefore, $x$-limits are $-2\\le x\\le 0.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/5b/5bafe1d8cd35eec83aba3a9a24f777340a09f824919d4cedc5879c774e30cf5e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}x\\le y\\le 5-{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0dce35b7-572a-54b4-af44-152ecca7b2e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0dce35b7-572a-54b4-af44-152ecca7b2e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "0dce35b7-572a-54b4-af44-152ecca7b2e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c7f6ba16-5e4c-5134-9ee4-51804e3e92c0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7f6ba16-5e4c-5134-9ee4-51804e3e92c0" "answer-c7a488ab-7038-5a44-bd73-39ae0126a903" "answer-a522044d-f1ef-55e8-b1ee-f64410c9b0e1" "answer-533ce4cc-1308-5319-806a-167ee6b29c95" "answer-0eac9096-a6ad-5d5e-9517-5c0ac0728af5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7f6ba16-5e4c-5134-9ee4-51804e3e92c0" :answer/id #uuid "c7f6ba16-5e4c-5134-9ee4-51804e3e92c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7a488ab-7038-5a44-bd73-39ae0126a903" :answer/id #uuid "c7a488ab-7038-5a44-bd73-39ae0126a903" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{5-{x}^{2}}^{-x/2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a522044d-f1ef-55e8-b1ee-f64410c9b0e1" :answer/id #uuid "a522044d-f1ef-55e8-b1ee-f64410c9b0e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{5-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-x/2}^{0}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-533ce4cc-1308-5319-806a-167ee6b29c95" :answer/id #uuid "533ce4cc-1308-5319-806a-167ee6b29c95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-x/2}^{0}{\\int }_{-2}^{5-{x}^{2}}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0eac9096-a6ad-5d5e-9517-5c0ac0728af5" :answer/id #uuid "0eac9096-a6ad-5d5e-9517-5c0ac0728af5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-x/2}^{5-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-d50c6341-afab-531b-95d7-80980e2f8edd" :activity/id #uuid "d50c6341-afab-531b-95d7-80980e2f8edd" :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049997 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-e0bceb01-13f0-500a-a70e-d7c56dc36189" "step-ed3c1020-e19e-5ebc-82db-39b373afe65a" "step-b1d9c3ed-33de-5684-8d99-d7f5f9b0c7e9" "step-f943e97a-19e3-5eb0-b644-0fd2bcdae89f" "step-09dc735b-be54-5f55-a40c-b3de9cb3c25f"] :activity/first-step "step-e0bceb01-13f0-500a-a70e-d7c56dc36189" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-e0bceb01-13f0-500a-a70e-d7c56dc36189" :step/id #uuid "e0bceb01-13f0-500a-a70e-d7c56dc36189" :step/content 17592186351293 :step/next "step-ed3c1020-e19e-5ebc-82db-39b373afe65a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ed3c1020-e19e-5ebc-82db-39b373afe65a" :step/id #uuid "ed3c1020-e19e-5ebc-82db-39b373afe65a" :step/content 17592186351295 :step/next "step-b1d9c3ed-33de-5684-8d99-d7f5f9b0c7e9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b1d9c3ed-33de-5684-8d99-d7f5f9b0c7e9" :step/id #uuid "b1d9c3ed-33de-5684-8d99-d7f5f9b0c7e9" :step/content 17592186351369 :step/next "step-f943e97a-19e3-5eb0-b644-0fd2bcdae89f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f943e97a-19e3-5eb0-b644-0fd2bcdae89f" :step/id #uuid "f943e97a-19e3-5eb0-b644-0fd2bcdae89f" :step/content "question-61556a8f-0a92-5ecd-8c20-5948347f1756" :step/next "step-09dc735b-be54-5f55-a40c-b3de9cb3c25f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-09dc735b-be54-5f55-a40c-b3de9cb3c25f" :step/id #uuid "09dc735b-be54-5f55-a40c-b3de9cb3c25f" :step/content 17592186268853 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078450 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b558035c-db2a-5cb8-b598-445d3a220fdc" "question-7f36415e-84fb-5eb9-a3cb-42107c1ff469"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078317 :question/problem "What is the value of $7\\times \\sqrt[3]{-8}?$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, we first evaluate the value of the radical, and we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\times \\sqrt[3]{-8} & = \\\\ 7\\times \\sqrt[3]{(-2)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-2)} & = \\\\ 7\\times \\sqrt[3]{(-2)^{3}} & = \\\\ 7\\times (-2) & = \\\\ -14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078451 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8d68d1bd-9a61-5ae6-b9dd-a03d4f1484bc" "question-442c94f9-fe8b-546a-8c5a-f7942d124959" "question-833d1e11-5a78-5410-a7b5-c29285538e0f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078318 :question/problem "Find the value of the expression $\\sqrt{ (3^4+7^2-2) \\div 2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{({3}^{4}+{7}^{2}-2)÷2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(81+49-2)÷2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(130-2)÷2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{128÷2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{64} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{8\\cdot 8} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{8}^{2}} & = \\\\ 8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078452 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1c3c665d-7d8f-59dd-ac27-822edd107571" "question-e0ce2975-3d0e-5df0-82ca-80c1fcc3d613" "question-c552807e-b3ea-557b-87d6-077887001adc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078319 :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\sqrt{((5^2-4) \\div 3)-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations,\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses, and\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents.\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(({5}^{2}-4)÷3)-2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{((25-4)÷3)-2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(21÷3)-2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{7-2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078453 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4ae171d6-ef6c-513e-bb69-e2d14e0a590f" "question-3a5045e4-621f-54ed-8866-51a959850017" "question-531dca87-ee51-5a1e-9c95-aee21eccb0e1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078320 :question/worked-solution "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations,\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses, and\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents.\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{13\\times 2-5}+1 & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{26-5}+1 & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{21}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we approximate the value of the radical using a calculator:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{21} \\approx 4.582\\,576\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we approximate the value of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{21}+1 & ≈ \\\\ ‭‭‭2(4.582\\,576)+1 & = \\\\ 9.165\\,152+1 & = \\\\ 10.165\\,152 & ≈ \\\\ 10.165\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b558035c-db2a-5cb8-b598-445d3a220fdc" :question/id #uuid "b558035c-db2a-5cb8-b598-445d3a220fdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-99397" :question/problem "$\\sqrt{4÷({3}^{2}-5)}=$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4÷({3}^{2}-5)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4÷(9-5)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4÷4} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & = \\\\ 1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-719b405c-02b7-5abd-9fbb-e9f0c434b9e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-719b405c-02b7-5abd-9fbb-e9f0c434b9e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "719b405c-02b7-5abd-9fbb-e9f0c434b9e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-79c01a83-b5d6-5769-bd3e-9071be86c070" :answer-field/choices ["answer-79c01a83-b5d6-5769-bd3e-9071be86c070" "answer-98a03a70-0f68-53dd-8f66-007f630f4929" "answer-d06a0e38-f6cc-5b53-9560-3b91aa3818ba" "answer-b3221abf-3137-573d-a169-ef9108202aa1" "answer-c216d6d4-e126-5ff3-8529-49885caaa9eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79c01a83-b5d6-5769-bd3e-9071be86c070" :answer/id #uuid "79c01a83-b5d6-5769-bd3e-9071be86c070" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98a03a70-0f68-53dd-8f66-007f630f4929" :answer/id #uuid "98a03a70-0f68-53dd-8f66-007f630f4929" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d06a0e38-f6cc-5b53-9560-3b91aa3818ba" :answer/id #uuid "d06a0e38-f6cc-5b53-9560-3b91aa3818ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3221abf-3137-573d-a169-ef9108202aa1" :answer/id #uuid "b3221abf-3137-573d-a169-ef9108202aa1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c216d6d4-e126-5ff3-8529-49885caaa9eb" :answer/id #uuid "c216d6d4-e126-5ff3-8529-49885caaa9eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7f36415e-84fb-5eb9-a3cb-42107c1ff469" :question/id #uuid "7f36415e-84fb-5eb9-a3cb-42107c1ff469" :question/math-academy-id "q-69087" :question/problem "What is the value of $(-2)\\sqrt{16}+3.3?$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, we first evaluate the value of the radical, and we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)\\sqrt{16}+3.3 & = \\\\ (-2)\\sqrt{4\\cdot 4}+3.3 & = \\\\ (-2)\\sqrt{{4}^{2}}+3.3 & = \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 4+3.3 & = \\\\ -8+3.3 & = \\\\ -4.7\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7e959a89-441f-583a-854e-03c006a6758c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7e959a89-441f-583a-854e-03c006a6758c" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e959a89-441f-583a-854e-03c006a6758c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bdedfb87-055e-58be-bf18-5385a24456b8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-05ebacb8-9e43-5ec9-a362-cfa8e5abaed4" "answer-070bae9f-3db5-5ef3-b55e-539a24848bd2" "answer-bdedfb87-055e-58be-bf18-5385a24456b8" "answer-3a727df6-305f-5665-bd84-eb6f1b0aa8fc" "answer-9f3ce23d-90cc-55e2-b4a2-d4cb56bdab74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05ebacb8-9e43-5ec9-a362-cfa8e5abaed4" :answer/id #uuid "05ebacb8-9e43-5ec9-a362-cfa8e5abaed4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-070bae9f-3db5-5ef3-b55e-539a24848bd2" :answer/id #uuid "070bae9f-3db5-5ef3-b55e-539a24848bd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdedfb87-055e-58be-bf18-5385a24456b8" :answer/id #uuid "bdedfb87-055e-58be-bf18-5385a24456b8" :a