tion.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-95c5b8ec-4e6b-57a2-adac-a57f9e64e01e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-95c5b8ec-4e6b-57a2-adac-a57f9e64e01e" :answer-field/id #uuid "95c5b8ec-4e6b-57a2-adac-a57f9e64e01e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e9c733b7-4ea2-53cb-a035-50bbf32b89b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-980de0e0-baed-55bd-b152-8acd4625867b" "answer-e9c733b7-4ea2-53cb-a035-50bbf32b89b5" "answer-2f5c89c3-07b7-5df3-a433-2fc26cf926fd" "answer-9adfb39f-6f5a-530a-b68a-6f8e62c7bc27" "answer-c1672645-f8a9-5c32-9e35-d4a4bef96882"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-980de0e0-baed-55bd-b152-8acd4625867b" :answer/id #uuid "980de0e0-baed-55bd-b152-8acd4625867b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9c733b7-4ea2-53cb-a035-50bbf32b89b5" :answer/id #uuid "e9c733b7-4ea2-53cb-a035-50bbf32b89b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-‭2.236" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f5c89c3-07b7-5df3-a433-2fc26cf926fd" :answer/id #uuid "2f5c89c3-07b7-5df3-a433-2fc26cf926fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9.905" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9adfb39f-6f5a-530a-b68a-6f8e62c7bc27" :answer/id #uuid "9adfb39f-6f5a-530a-b68a-6f8e62c7bc27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "‭-2.045" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1672645-f8a9-5c32-9e35-d4a4bef96882" :answer/id #uuid "c1672645-f8a9-5c32-9e35-d4a4bef96882" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "‭2.578" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-531dca87-ee51-5a1e-9c95-aee21eccb0e1" :question/id #uuid "531dca87-ee51-5a1e-9c95-aee21eccb0e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-171401" :question/problem "Rounded to $3$ decimal places, $\\sqrt{6+4\\times 2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6+4\\times 2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{6+8} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we approximate the value of $\\sqrt{14}$ using a calculator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & ≈‭‭‭3.742\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40f14f8c-bc9b-58e2-900b-314d7347ebbd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40f14f8c-bc9b-58e2-900b-314d7347ebbd" :answer-field/id #uuid "40f14f8c-bc9b-58e2-900b-314d7347ebbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b824ae97-0782-520b-98c2-6828b4f4b7ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b824ae97-0782-520b-98c2-6828b4f4b7ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b824ae97-0782-520b-98c2-6828b4f4b7ef" :answer/id #uuid "b824ae97-0782-520b-98c2-6828b4f4b7ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.742" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079185 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\sqrt{2\\times 4-6}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS. In terms of the order of operations: \n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nTherefore, following the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2\\times 4-6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{8-6} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we approximate the value of $\\sqrt{2}$ using a calculator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & ≈‭‭‭1.414\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186077888 :answer-field/correct 17592186077890 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079667 :tutorial/content "Suppose we need to evaluate an expression like\n\n$$\n1+\\sqrt{4},\n$$\n\nwhere we have a square root sign. What would be the order of operations? It turns out that the root signs ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) can be thought of as parentheses. \nIn other words, we find the value under the square root first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\sqrt{4} & = \\\\ 1+2 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly, let's evaluate $\\big(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27}\\big) \\div 2$.\n\nTo evaluate expressions with radicals, we use PEMDAS as before, but with the following additional rules:\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nFollowing the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27})÷2 & = \\\\ (1-\\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}})÷2 & = \\\\ (1-(-3))÷2 & = \\\\ 4÷2 & = \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186079665 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe first thing to realize is that there is an invisible multiplication symbol here. The above expression is short for\n\n$$\n-3\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\\,\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhen multiplying a radical by a number, we usually omit the additional multiplication symbol.\n\nNow, to simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS.  In terms of the order of operations:\n\n- Expressions underneath radicals are treated as if they are contained within parentheses (the **P** in **P**EMDAS), so we always deal with this first.\n\n- Radicals themselves are treated as exponents (the **E** in P**E**MDAS).\n\nTherefore, following PEMDAS, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{({2}^{4}-1)÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{(16-1)÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{15÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! \n\nTo get a numerical approximation, we need to use a calculator. In this case, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\sqrt{5} = -3\\times \\sqrt{5} \\approx -6.708\\,204\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078630 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186396437 :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3" :question/id #uuid "4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334797" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}=(2.5)^{3}=15.625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.125}{15.625}=0.008.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈0.008.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-817219d2-4fac-5374-9c4a-6822ef9e7011"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-817219d2-4fac-5374-9c4a-6822ef9e7011" :answer-field/id #uuid "817219d2-4fac-5374-9c4a-6822ef9e7011" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2414084d-978d-5852-9a75-b14f36d5dc64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2414084d-978d-5852-9a75-b14f36d5dc64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2414084d-978d-5852-9a75-b14f36d5dc64" :answer/id #uuid "2414084d-978d-5852-9a75-b14f36d5dc64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.008" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5f5a0a27-74df-55fb-b257-c512fcb73cb8" :activity/id #uuid "5f5a0a27-74df-55fb-b257-c512fcb73cb8" :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051034 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-054123fc-51ab-5f29-abe7-820e908c72d0" "step-24fbfd65-2e32-5d51-9949-234d1117d72d" "step-d1344963-f111-577e-9b39-d9ba8d2a6219" "step-017a4cef-d60d-58c4-aa28-d0d444164350" "step-110acc7d-2f94-5af9-b727-c5d13e21f510"] :activity/first-step "step-054123fc-51ab-5f29-abe7-820e908c72d0" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-054123fc-51ab-5f29-abe7-820e908c72d0" :step/id #uuid "054123fc-51ab-5f29-abe7-820e908c72d0" :step/content 17592186221975 :step/next "step-24fbfd65-2e32-5d51-9949-234d1117d72d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-24fbfd65-2e32-5d51-9949-234d1117d72d" :step/id #uuid "24fbfd65-2e32-5d51-9949-234d1117d72d" :step/content 17592186221986 :step/next "step-d1344963-f111-577e-9b39-d9ba8d2a6219" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d1344963-f111-577e-9b39-d9ba8d2a6219" :step/id #uuid "d1344963-f111-577e-9b39-d9ba8d2a6219" :step/content 17592186396437 :step/next "step-017a4cef-d60d-58c4-aa28-d0d444164350" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-017a4cef-d60d-58c4-aa28-d0d444164350" :step/id #uuid "017a4cef-d60d-58c4-aa28-d0d444164350" :step/content "question-4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3" :step/next "step-110acc7d-2f94-5af9-b727-c5d13e21f510" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-110acc7d-2f94-5af9-b727-c5d13e21f510" :step/id #uuid "110acc7d-2f94-5af9-b727-c5d13e21f510" :step/content 17592186308709 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186184775 :question/answer-fields 17592186183259] {:db/id 17592186184852 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354515 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186369707 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416358 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416323 :question/problem "Find the distance between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269233 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269228 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354518 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269229 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269227 :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184775 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -9 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}10 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186269235 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get $3{x}_{1}=4{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 25\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}25 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186392361 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}17 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186416357 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354522 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064325 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2bee595e-f4bd-59ff-8622-53fe58115cfe" "question-b2c7f8cb-2d06-5a06-8c22-c48f3e03b4b4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063960 :question/problem "Factor $x^2 + 12x + 36.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 =  (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\nx^2 + 12x + 36.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+12x+{6}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{6}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064326 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-380d2267-0a37-5388-bacc-f5df7afa8e5c" "question-94bbfbab-f0f9-56d8-b645-4168ecf299d7" "question-f18cf588-d703-5104-bc39-415b94a76959"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063961 :question/problem "Factor $p^2 - 10p + 25.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\np^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} p + a^2 =  (p-{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\np^2 - 10p + 25.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-10p+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}-2\\cdot 5p+{5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{5}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064327 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3ab2e3ce-42ef-5550-a256-e8688e7ed48b" "question-33ad6f1c-10b4-54e8-b066-192c2a7ae636"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063962 :question/problem "Factor the expression $y^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 =  (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\ny^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2bee595e-f4bd-59ff-8622-53fe58115cfe" :question/id #uuid "2bee595e-f4bd-59ff-8622-53fe58115cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-111601" :question/problem "Factor ${p}^{2}+49+14p.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}+2ap+{a}^{2}=(p+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{p}^{2}+49+14p={p}^{2}+14p+49.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}+14p+{7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2}+2\\cdot 7p+{7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=7.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+7)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bcb82e72-0818-572b-80d9-7cc60a35cab2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bcb82e72-0818-572b-80d9-7cc60a35cab2" :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb82e72-0818-572b-80d9-7cc60a35cab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1ffa3d12-0822-568b-8e93-b5d8b1c6fd9d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33223ce6-b3e6-59ce-b2da-d2c0df8d69d9" "answer-908a0c93-c943-5bea-bc33-fdb5a44e6b93" "answer-f0b14ade-f921-56fb-a6eb-14b2dabaa664" "answer-1ffa3d12-0822-568b-8e93-b5d8b1c6fd9d" "answer-3edc24e9-45c2-5aa0-bc23-986f203c4812"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33223ce6-b3e6-59ce-b2da-d2c0df8d69d9" :answer/id #uuid "33223ce6-b3e6-59ce-b2da-d2c0df8d69d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(p+14)^{2}" :