tep-dd5c2617-10d3-5d03-a33c-d87b258640bb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dd5c2617-10d3-5d03-a33c-d87b258640bb" :step/id #uuid "dd5c2617-10d3-5d03-a33c-d87b258640bb" :step/content "question-da6d460c-dde7-5fc1-ac50-c5ee168f1351" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186161198 :answer-field/choices 17592186161200] [:db/retract 17592186161198 :answer-field/choices 17592186161201] [:db/retract 17592186161198 :answer-field/choices 17592186161202] [:db/retract 17592186161198 :answer-field/choices 17592186161203] [:db/retract 17592186161198 :answer-field/choices 17592186161199] {:db/id 17592186162001 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a5a77e1a-841f-57c5-8990-3f339cc0802e" "question-90278635-fce6-5765-ae39-6533399724f4" "question-3249d5d6-dafe-59e8-a2d4-ffdd59e29afa" "question-95801bee-5916-59b8-993a-e9a5631d930a" "question-68f249e7-d40f-5592-ac60-248ad19377c7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161793 :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n![](images/13/137e06758f63232479a573ffd7c7c68cffd832d8fac22036bce04e9968ad7c4a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $7.$\n\n![](images/d5/d500063d0885dc2437d83754d8e54d81740a8b6fd1855416e51dab2577cb27a6.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162002 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d092f972-98cf-5c56-8546-b76547cbb36a" "question-a54b50d2-00a6-51c8-a8ca-b8464cbdd7da" "question-7c410d24-cb7b-59e5-b706-34c806e9c72e" "question-7a19bcdd-15af-5ef0-a245-ee1a2c98862d" "question-ed1f11b0-312f-5ca9-81fd-de20b37867fa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161794 :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n![](images/d6/d63a97b931fedb6b001f590134b448dd2222f33606725343486064cec5e85036.png)" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=1,7,13.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $1,7,$ and $13.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4efdc7b16747f33e48be7c73369e4f881177d6faba19d02bc832bfa0e916278.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162004 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d0fd2e28-4af0-53c5-9c68-348af97ab34c" "question-90f3bc1d-94f7-5716-a7c9-9b34d22c275c" "question-72c83079-fa51-5573-830e-81ba6d3220b1" "question-c069136c-9e24-5901-bcc3-c8fd76fb24b5" "question-5361d7e0-4877-5c07-8e5e-b4d8b7ac0426"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161796 :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n![](images/a3/a3de8797a3b489ada861edbebb5de935dc5c840dafc509bf2b41ad093d7cc2b4.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x\\leq \\pi$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos x}{2} = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only $x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ lies within the interval $\\left[0, \\pi \\right].$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0, \\pi.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162003 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cc6f0c6d-fbfc-515f-9faf-cdb2e36bd29a" "question-3564d8be-ba15-5300-bf05-043e2a7c4f77" "question-04da65b8-370f-5d43-9d9a-986d048126c3" "question-fed1e82d-9029-565f-bbd7-a48e9974d84e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161795 :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n![](images/9e/9ec101ca3c5052991372544517cd3d497b6d58f6df7b81084d2fd16a9c74ac89.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}(x-1)^2.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2(x-1) \\\\  & =3(x-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n3(x-1)= 0\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThis critical point is within the interval $[0,2],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0, 2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1, 2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}(0-1)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)^{2}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(2-1)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(0 ) = f( 2) = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $0$ and $2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a5a77e1a-841f-57c5-8990-3f339cc0802e" :question/id #uuid "a5a77e1a-841f-57c5-8990-3f339cc0802e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95180" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa2638a96ae91d0b2a8a0d1216e8256c7823c231c5ceecd9a4b3fd14c239ed53.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function $f(x)$ shown above. What is the mode of $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that $f(x)$ has a single global maximum at $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $4.$\n\n![](images/52/52c2514b1970553a6b4f80138e4baa6b8384d482caa447b95bd07dde5c252d11.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c27f59c-b990-53cc-b76a-6b1142b13698"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c27f59c-b990-53cc-b76a-6b1142b13698" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c27f59c-b990-53cc-b76a-6b1142b13698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-de04b441-5016-544f-8840-fa984523ad76" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a66ef2ce-98c2-5fcd-a1b2-a5d63125256e" "answer-e2ea766d-0da4-5e41-9554-9431f9c78903" "answer-de04b441-5016-544f-8840-fa984523ad76" "answer-8d8dc4ca-9317-550c-b503-c8f42f4dcf68" "answer-d507d780-5b25-56d1-8f1d-0f78a564827d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a66ef2ce-98c2-5fcd-a1b2-a5d63125256e" :answer/id #uuid "a66ef2ce-98c2-5fcd-a1b2-a5d63125256e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2ea766d-0da4-5e41-9554-9431f9c78903" :answer/id #uuid "e2ea766d-0da4-5e