f $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=5,10.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $5$ and $10.$\n\n![](images/d7/d70343741f05f43de9db3624eda40e9ed003819db2cb54e37a3392a2887362da.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5a04a5a-a8d6-5c62-87cd-5e8f54bf2132"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5a04a5a-a8d6-5c62-87cd-5e8f54bf2132" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5a04a5a-a8d6-5c62-87cd-5e8f54bf2132" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b52ae7c6-b431-5307-9b57-d82b0e56d1bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5032d741-1f8c-5f0c-8458-effa5f7e8be7" "answer-98a9463a-96c7-5aa9-84aa-7f360a327ccc" "answer-b52ae7c6-b431-5307-9b57-d82b0e56d1bd" "answer-147cb54a-676f-5177-b4f1-a22804f51ecb" "answer-cf93e0e9-4170-591e-a2aa-50d7dae2c4b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5032d741-1f8c-5f0c-8458-effa5f7e8be7" :answer/id #uuid "5032d741-1f8c-5f0c-8458-effa5f7e8be7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2,10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98a9463a-96c7-5aa9-84aa-7f360a327ccc" :answer/id #uuid "98a9463a-96c7-5aa9-84aa-7f360a327ccc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2,5,7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b52ae7c6-b431-5307-9b57-d82b0e56d1bd" :answer/id #uuid "b52ae7c6-b431-5307-9b57-d82b0e56d1bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5,10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-147cb54a-676f-5177-b4f1-a22804f51ecb" :answer/id #uuid "147cb54a-676f-5177-b4f1-a22804f51ecb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5,7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf93e0e9-4170-591e-a2aa-50d7dae2c4b9" :answer/id #uuid "cf93e0e9-4170-591e-a2aa-50d7dae2c4b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5,7,10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ed1f11b0-312f-5ca9-81fd-de20b37867fa" :question/id #uuid "ed1f11b0-312f-5ca9-81fd-de20b37867fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-126294" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6f2ce8adb8c1b35c2fd4928805f486920228b913c3a7fc18ddc60b26a8e8fcd.png)\n\nLet $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown above. What are the modes of $X?$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we can tell that the global maxima of $f(x)$ are at $x=5,7.$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $5$ and $7.$\n\n![](images/59/59b72efa72dd48bc2fb74fed5f795341f59af377af0756965abab9d23390d468.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d875bb2-b179-54eb-ac35-d4ab00c3557d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d875bb2-b179-54eb-ac35-d4ab00c3557d" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d875bb2-b179-54eb-ac35-d4ab00c3557d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-04657e13-e69b-5894-ac2e-427c99373af7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f13b64d-2dce-53bf-8ff3-5482b2db4c08" "answer-bc717a72-c626-5254-aaa8-7c5effd6a89b" "answer-21f6d952-ec75-52e8-8858-10b25f61865c" "answer-67afab5f-20f7-541f-84cf-2494975f72ea" "answer-04657e13-e69b-5894-ac2e-427c99373af7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f13b64d-2dce-53bf-8ff3-5482b2db4c08" :answer/id #uuid "6f13b64d-2dce-53bf-8ff3-5482b2db4c08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2,5,7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc717a72-c626-5254-aaa8-7c5effd6a89b" :answer/id #uuid "bc717a72-c626-5254-aaa8-7c5effd6a89b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2,5,9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21f6d952-ec75-52e8-8858-10b25f61865c" :answer/id #uuid "21f6d952-ec75-52e8-8858-10b25f61865c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4,6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67afab5f-20f7-541f-84cf-2494975f72ea" :answer/id #uuid "67afab5f-20f7-541f-84cf-2494975f72ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04657e13-e69b-5894-ac2e-427c99373af7" :answer/id #uuid "04657e13-e69b-5894-ac2e-427c99373af7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5,7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d0fd2e28-4af0-53c5-9c68-348af97ab34c" :question/id #uuid "d0fd2e28-4af0-53c5-9c68-348af97ab34c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95348" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 5$ is $f(x)=\\frac{3}{100}(5+4x-{x}^{2}).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{100}(4-2x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=2$ is in the interval $[0,5],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,5.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 0-{0}^{2})=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2})=\\frac{27}{100} \\\\ f(5) & =\\frac{3}{100}(5+4\\cdot 5-{5}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{27}{100}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf38b3b7-889a-5d4b-add6-1de81acf50be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf38b3b7-889a-5d4b-add6-1de81acf50be" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf38b3b7-889a-5d4b-add6-1de81acf50be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b5f18087-3589-5bb1-b55b-ec1c1e75de92" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c74131b8-caab-5a3e-a0d9-32a36917054f" "answer-b5f18087-3589-5bb1-b55b-ec1c1e75de92" "answer-58950bbd-e3ee-5c07-b30b-ddb473d62ab9" "answer-20ec2c09-e6fa-5afa-a648-fb02946dddda" "answer-0eb7b064-6ea7-5770-979d-8abdcb24a1aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c74131b8-caab-5a3e-a0d9-32a36917054f" :answer/id #uuid "c74131b8-caab-5a3e-a0d9-32a36917054f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5f18087-3589-5bb1-b55b-ec1c1e75de92" :answer/id #uuid "b5f18087-3589-5bb1-b55b-ec1c1e75de92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58950bbd-e3ee-5c07-b30b-ddb473d62ab9" :answer/id #uuid "58950bbd-e3ee-5c07-b30b-ddb473d62ab9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20ec2c09-e6fa-5afa-a648-fb02946dddda" :answer/id #uuid "20ec2c09-e6fa-5afa-a648-fb02946dddda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{27}{100}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0eb7b064-6ea7-5770-979d-8abdcb24a1aa" :answer/id #uuid "0eb7b064-6ea7-5770-979d-8abdcb24a1aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-90f3bc1d-94f7-5716-a7c9-9b34d22c275c" :question/id #uuid "90f3bc1d-94f7-5716-a7c9-9b34d22c275c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126323" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=3{e}^{-3x}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-9{e}^{-3x}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f.$ Therefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d51fc38-d3aa-53d3-aca8-4f3a38a9dad3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d51fc38-d3aa-53d3-aca8-4f3a38a9dad3" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d51fc38-d3aa-53d3-aca8-4f3a38a9dad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d844503e-26ef-5f85-b233-34d70948d178" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7442236d-8264-5a32-a3e2-b465b840d103" "answer-d844503e-26ef-5f85-b233-34d70948d178" "answer-ca5d22cd-920b-5434-88ab-9c1bd372a226" "answer-1082a3b2-06e8-5365-82b5-32a2d22cab5a" "answer-171293e6-ca8b-5e7f-9ecc-891ec0c09d2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7442236d-8264-5a32-a3e2-b465b840d103" :answer/id #uuid "7442236d-8264-5a32-a3e2-b465b840d103" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d844503e-26ef-5f85-b233-34d70948d178" :answer/id #uuid "d844503e-26ef-5f85-b233-34d70948d178" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca5d22cd-920b-5434-88ab-9c1bd372a226" :answer/id #uuid "ca5d22cd-920b-5434-88ab-9c1bd372a226" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1082a3b2-06e8-5365-82b5-32a2d22cab5a" :answer/id #uuid "1082a3b2-06e8-5365-82b5-32a2d22cab5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-171293e6-ca8b-5e7f-9ecc-891ec0c09d2c" :answer/id #uuid "171293e6-ca8b-5e7f-9ecc-891ec0c09d2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-72c83079-fa51-5573-830e-81ba6d3220b1" :question/id #uuid "72c83079-fa51-5573-830e-81ba6d3220b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95328" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,2.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(2)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0f670097-2378-5e08-98c6-e7db12d192b2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f670097-2378-5e08-98c6-e7db12d192b2" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f670097-2378-5e08-98c6-e7db12d192b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-454ccc01-cdb3-5155-8a6e-65477458f869" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3cc505f2-78f1-5c70-88f4-d6032b9a5c09" "answer-393f65ce-23ad-5614-aa05-25d2d89acbb6" "answer-454ccc01-cdb3-5155-8a6e-65477458f869" "answer-b01250a0-7f57-5f95-bfbf-f046438b94d5" "answer-cfd25a84-48d7-5c69-a712-cc443b6851fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cc505f2-78f1-5c70-88f4-d6032b9a5c09" :answer/id #uuid "3cc505f2-78f1-5c70-88f4-d6032b9a5c09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-393f65ce-23ad-5614-aa05-25d2d89acbb6" :answer/id #uuid "393f65ce-23ad-5614-aa05-25d2d89acbb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-454ccc01-cdb3-5155-8a6e-65477458f869" :answer/id #uuid "454ccc01-cdb3-5155-8a6e-65477458f869" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b01250a0-7f57-5f95-bfbf-f046438b94d5" :answer/id #uuid "b01250a0-7f57-5f95-bfbf-f046438b94d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfd25a84-48d7-5c69-a712-cc443b6851fc" :answer/id #uuid "cfd25a84-48d7-5c69-a712-cc443b6851fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c069136c-9e24-5901-bcc3-c8fd76fb24b5" :question/id #uuid "c069136c-9e24-5901-bcc3-c8fd76fb24b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126721" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(1+x)^{3}}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions of  ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoint $x=0.$ So, this is the only critical point.\n\nSince ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for $x>0,$ then $f(0)$ is the global maximum of $f(x).$\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-20ca85d7-4841-50ea-a46d-18e2741e2de1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-20ca85d7-4841-50ea-a46d-18e2741e2de1" :answer-field/id #uuid "20ca85d7-4841-50ea-a46d-18e2741e2de1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ce7a1336-f3e9-5ebe-b7db-72ffe18ae7da" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ce7a1336-f3e9-5ebe-b7db-72ffe18ae7da" "answer-b10db118-036b-5d52-8ae6-0ee7ed64170f" "answer-60d2f142-9346-53de-b0c8-157b88f8834d" "answer-156d8fb9-a3ef-5cf0-a116-cde0d34d5ab6" "answer-961db6b5-e10b-5c17-9882-c4b693893797"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce7a1336-f3e9-5ebe-b7db-72ffe18ae7da" :answer/id #uuid "ce7a1336-f3e9-5ebe-b7db-72ffe18ae7da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b10db118-036b-5d52-8ae6-0ee7ed64170f" :answer/id #uuid "b10db118-036b-5d52-8ae6-0ee7ed64170f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60d2f142-9346-53de-b0c8-157b88f8834d" :answer/id #uuid "60d2f142-9346-53de-b0c8-157b88f8834d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-156d8fb9-a3ef-5cf0-a116-cde0d34d5ab6" :answer/id #uuid "156d8fb9-a3ef-5cf0-a116-cde0d34d5ab6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-961db6b5-e10b-5c17-9882-c4b693893797" :answer/id #uuid "961db6b5-e10b-5c17-9882-c4b693893797" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5361d7e0-4877-5c07-8e5e-b4d8b7ac0426" :question/id #uuid "5361d7e0-4877-5c07-8e5e-b4d8b7ac0426" :question/math-academy-id "q-126179" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{x}^{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{9}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9}x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThe point $x=0$ is in the interval $[0,3],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {0}^{2}=0 \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(3)=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf31510e-14a1-5ed8-a2ac-a70c7049efd6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf31510e-14a1-5ed8-a2ac-a70c7049efd6" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf31510e-14a1-5ed8-a2ac-a70c7049efd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a109c617-97d0-5f35-b138-970c2477e232" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0127daaf-87d8-5cdd-bdc6-723f2bd9ef53" "answer-fc8f3418-48fb-5e2d-982c-77fd5497ed14" "answer-ce66b1bb-2965-5a68-ba8d-76a5055610ec" "answer-a109c617-97d0-5f35-b138-970c2477e232" "answer-5aec8f18-e1a4-58de-8c20-9808604c0760"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0127daaf-87d8-5cdd-bdc6-723f2bd9ef53" :answer/id #uuid "0127daaf-87d8-5cdd-bdc6-723f2bd9ef53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc8f3418-48fb-5e2d-982c-77fd5497ed14" :answer/id #uuid "fc8f3418-48fb-5e2d-982c-77fd5497ed14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce66b1bb-2965-5a68-ba8d-76a5055610ec" :answer/id #uuid "ce66b1bb-2965-5a68-ba8d-76a5055610ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a109c617-97d0-5f35-b138-970c2477e232" :answer/id #uuid "a109c617-97d0-5f35-b138-970c2477e232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aec8f18-e1a4-58de-8c20-9808604c0760" :answer/id #uuid "5aec8f18-e1a4-58de-8c20-9808604c0760" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162651 :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{128}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}), & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-2\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{15}{128}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{15}{128}(8x-4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{32}(2x-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{32}x(2-{x}^{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{32}x(2-{x}^{2})=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-2,2],$ so we consider them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm \\sqrt{2},\\pm 2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =0 \\\\ f(-\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{4})=\\frac{15}{128}(8-4)=\\frac{15}{32} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(0)}^{2}-{(0)}^{4})=0 \\\\ f(\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{15}{128}(4{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{2})}^{4})=\\frac{15}{128}(8-4)=\\frac{15}{32} \\\\ f(2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-\\sqrt{2})=f(\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{15}{32}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161198 :answer-field/correct "answer-71db68f4-8250-546a-b7d2-c4932f56d175" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fb7e527d-fac9-5aad-b55d-08c430d951b2" "answer-115e77fb-e02b-5237-b31f-12a56efafa90" "answer-ee932987-4d59-5b7f-8b0d-6b1eb79047d4" "answer-a4af405c-2d3b-5a9c-a6cd-6a91a01e3368" "answer-71db68f4-8250-546a-b7d2-c4932f56d175"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb7e527d-fac9-5aad-b55d-08c430d951b2" :answer/id #uuid "fb7e527d-fac9-5aad-b55d-08c430d951b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-115e77fb-e02b-5237-b31f-12a56efafa90" :answer/id #uuid "115e77fb-e02b-5237-b31f-12a56efafa90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2,0,2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee932987-4d59-5b7f-8b0d-6b1eb79047d4" :answer/id #uuid "ee932987-4d59-5b7f-8b0d-6b1eb79047d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2,2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4af405c-2d3b-5a9c-a6cd-6a91a01e3368" :answer/id #uuid "a4af405c-2d3b-5a9c-a6cd-6a91a01e3368" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2},0,\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71db68f4-8250-546a-b7d2-c4932f56d175" :answer/id #uuid "71db68f4-8250-546a-b7d2-c4932f56d175" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cc6f0c6d-fbfc-515f-9faf-cdb2e36bd29a" :question/id #uuid "cc6f0c6d-fbfc-515f-9faf-cdb2e36bd29a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95380" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}, & -1\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is $f(x)=\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =10{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n10{x}^{3}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nThis critical point is within the interval $[-1,1],$ so we keep it.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-1,1.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-1,0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-1)^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ f(0) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {0}^{4}}{2}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{5\\cdot {1}^{4}}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0846b1e1-5974-53e1-a637-306e6e3e8f94"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0846b1e1-5974-53e1-a637-306e6e3e8f94" :answer-field/id #uuid "0846b1e1-5974-53e1-a637-306e6e3e8f94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5650a8d9-5536-5945-b509-63800912cc45" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88c4e86f-2e4e-50ce-94cf-69673f2e804b" "answer-aec77f18-46f9-56bc-9c5f-171f74af8827" "answer-5650a8d9-5536-5945-b509-63800912cc45" "answer-7fb401a5-847f-54af-abdb-c557eab69963" "answer-5eefcdf0-d372-5d82-907e-206da45b0d49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88c4e86f-2e4e-50ce-94cf-69673f2e804b" :answer/id #uuid "88c4e86f-2e4e-50ce-94cf-69673f2e804b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aec77f18-46f9-56bc-9c5f-171f74af8827" :answer/id #uuid "aec77f18-46f9-56bc-9c5f-171f74af8827" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,0,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5650a8d9-5536-5945-b509-63800912cc45" :answer/id #uuid "5650a8d9-5536-5945-b509-63800912cc45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fb401a5-847f-54af-abdb-c557eab69963" :answer/id #uuid "7fb401a5-847f-54af-abdb-c557eab69963" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5eefcdf0-d372-5d82-907e-206da45b0d49" :answer/id #uuid "5eefcdf0-d372-5d82-907e-206da45b0d49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3564d8be-ba15-5300-bf05-043e2a7c4f77" :question/id #uuid "3564d8be-ba15-5300-bf05-043e2a7c4f77" :question/math-academy-id "q-97025" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}, & -2\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-2\\le x\\le 2$ is $f(x)=\\frac{(2-x)(x+1)^{2}}{8}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{-(x+1)^{2}+2(2-x)(x+1)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(-x-1+4-2x)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(x+1)(1-x)}{8}\\,⟹\\,x=-1,1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-2,2],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=-2,2.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=-2,-1,1,2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =\\frac{(2+2)(-2+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(-1) & =\\frac{(2+1)(-1+1)^{2}}{8}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{(2-1)(1+1)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{(2-2)(2+1)^{2}}{8}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-2)=f(1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-2$ and $1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89711e9c-e116-50a7-ae9d-6fbf9295b7f8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89711e9c-e116-50a7-ae9d-6fbf9295b7f8" :answer-field/id #uuid "89711e9c-e116-50a7-ae9d-6fbf9295b7f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-803b79a6-bd27-5019-a0ca-6c717c883e01" :answer-field/choices ["answer-616aa6a5-4b43-534d-821a-bbdbc35c16f3" "answer-8002f0e0-b8eb-52ab-98a1-fb52f05547d2" "answer-d675c4d0-7a79-5251-b122-3bcefc69e692" "answer-803b79a6-bd27-5019-a0ca-6c717c883e01" "answer-91229bac-46a3-5f6c-aba9-119cb3512ae8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-616aa6a5-4b43-534d-821a-bbdbc35c16f3" :answer/id #uuid "616aa6a5-4b43-534d-821a-bbdbc35c16f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8002f0e0-b8eb-52ab-98a1-fb52f05547d2" :answer/id #uuid "8002f0e0-b8eb-52ab-98a1-fb52f05547d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,1,2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d675c4d0-7a79-5251-b122-3bcefc69e692" :answer/id #uuid "d675c4d0-7a79-5251-b122-3bcefc69e692" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2,-1,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-803b79a6-bd27-5019-a0ca-6c717c883e01" :answer/id #uuid "803b79a6-bd27-5019-a0ca-6c717c883e01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91229bac-46a3-5f6c-aba9-119cb3512ae8" :answer/id #uuid "91229bac-46a3-5f6c-aba9-119cb3512ae8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2,2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04da65b8-370f-5d43-9d9a-986d048126c3" :question/id #uuid "04da65b8-370f-5d43-9d9a-986d048126c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97023" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}, & 0\\le x\\le 8 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 8$ is $f(x)=\\frac{x(6-x)^{2}}{128}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(6-x)^{2}-2x(6-x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6-x)(6-x-2x)}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3(6-x)(2-x)}{128}\\,⟹\\,x=2,6.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[0,8],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,8.$ So, these are critical points as well.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,2,6,8:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{0(6-0)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2(6-2)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(6) & =\\frac{6(6-6)^{2}}{128}=0 \\\\ f(8) & =\\frac{8(6-8)^{2}}{128}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(2)=f(8)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $2$ and $8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f864eeb7-5c3a-520c-b5c5-87f43508e40a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f864eeb7-5c3a-520c-b5c5-87f43508e40a" :answer-field/id #uuid "f864eeb7-5c3a-520c-b5c5-87f43508e40a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c956b69f-1371-5064-85a7-92f548f1708c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2a39c9ca-304c-5dc6-bf0a-a3191df9d199" "answer-2fc1d53b-065c-5f28-baee-103cc2d04fe5" "answer-6c32fa51-116b-5e6b-8ad8-9c15a7a0762b" "answer-25cf873b-c561-529e-a150-dbd67081b794" "answer-c956b69f-1371-5064-85a7-92f548f1708c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a39c9ca-304c-5dc6-bf0a-a3191df9d199" :answer/id #uuid "2a39c9ca-304c-5dc6-bf0a-a3191df9d199" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0,2,6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fc1d53b-065c-5f28-baee-103cc2d04fe5" :answer/id #uuid "2fc1d53b-065c-5f28-baee-103cc2d04fe5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0,2,8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c32fa51-116b-5e6b-8ad8-9c15a7a0762b" :answer/id #uuid "6c32fa51-116b-5e6b-8ad8-9c15a7a0762b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2,6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25cf873b-c561-529e-a150-dbd67081b794" :answer/id #uuid "25cf873b-c561-529e-a150-dbd67081b794" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2,6,8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c956b69f-1371-5064-85a7-92f548f1708c" :answer/id #uuid "c956b69f-1371-5064-85a7-92f548f1708c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2,8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fed1e82d-9029-565f-bbd7-a48e9974d84e" :question/id #uuid "fed1e82d-9029-565f-bbd7-a48e9974d84e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127090" :question/problem "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2), & -1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ is $f(x)=-\\frac{96}{595}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{96}{595}(4{x}^{3}-4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{384}{595}x({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[-1,\\frac{3}{2}],$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=\\pm 1.$ So, these are critical points as well, but $-1$ is included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,\\pm 1,\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(0) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}-2)=\\frac{192}{595} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{96}{595}((1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}-2)=\\frac{288}{595} \\\\ f(\\frac{3}{2}) & =-\\frac{96}{595}({(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4}-2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2)=\\frac{138}{595}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(-1)=f(1)=\\frac{288}{595}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $-1$ and $1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68323756-6915-5a2a-b83f-b1d14e9cdd18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68323756-6915-5a2a-b83f-b1d14e9cdd18" :answer-field/id #uuid "68323756-6915-5a2a-b83f-b1d14e9cdd18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1a3281f8-9551-52d3-8d0c-c6b89b2d2548" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16ac1ac8-5d12-5b50-934c-de82720f574b" "answer-4dde1e24-43fb-5f71-b240-852e369c1dd1" "answer-1a3281f8-9551-52d3-8d0c-c6b89b2d2548" "answer-c0cdcb5b-73ea-5836-bf70-b1a7b83f23d3" "answer-22e163e2-c51b-5f8c-acce-093bdea2aa79"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16ac1ac8-5d12-5b50-934c-de82720f574b" :answer/id #uuid "16ac1ac8-5d12-5b50-934c-de82720f574b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dde1e24-43fb-5f71-b240-852e369c1dd1" :answer/id #uuid "4dde1e24-43fb-5f71-b240-852e369c1dd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,0,1,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a3281f8-9551-52d3-8d0c-c6b89b2d2548" :answer/id #uuid "1a3281f8-9551-52d3-8d0c-c6b89b2d2548" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0cdcb5b-73ea-5836-bf70-b1a7b83f23d3" :answer/id #uuid "c0cdcb5b-73ea-5836-bf70-b1a7b83f23d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22e163e2-c51b-5f8c-acce-093bdea2aa79" :answer/id #uuid "22e163e2-c51b-5f8c-acce-093bdea2aa79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0,1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163240 :tutorial/content "The **modes of a random variable $X$** with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nFor example, the modes of the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below are ${\\color{blue}5}$ and ${\\color{blue}9}$ (the values of $x$ that correspond to the global maxima):\n\n![](images/49/49e3f3c63c01ce5c00e9d2ff51bcd03d7516f7866f510ce091071a13a8496210.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163259 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose we are given the random variable $X$ with the probability density function shown below. \n\n![](images/ec/ec48a5d839f3d843fe4738707ec3d067a19db9b009f4bf29673d899b321248a3.svg)\n\nHow can we find the mode of $X$ algebraically? \n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ is $f(x) =2x.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are no solutions  of  $f'(x) = 0.$\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x = 0,1.$ So, these are critical points.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =2(0)=0 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf({\\color{blue}1}) =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is ${\\color{blue}1}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162179 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133800 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e65b2b16-1d73-53d7-b99e-0f6611b54099" "question-72a8e42a-c396-5007-82f3-4a7623931a98" "question-22478d60-239d-58ef-b70b-d84ab0dcc7e7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133799 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f16e5a65-b3a9-5e9e-afd2-4d31b6ccc499"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133608 :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-12 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133848 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bbdebe64-e032-577f-8298-161c9dc3fe78"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e65b2b16-1d73-53d7-b99e-0f6611b54099" :question/id #uuid "e65b2b16-1d73-53d7-b99e-0f6611b54099" :question/math-academy-id "q-40890" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46985715-006b-561e-9632-119e4caaef8f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46985715-006b-561e-9632-119e4caaef8f" :answer-field/id #uuid "46985715-006b-561e-9632-119e4caaef8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0a0ba0b0-2f62-5e59-80be-54a6ff21b8df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a150de1e-a1ab-51fb-b47b-78fa9b93d0f2" "answer-0a0ba0b0-2f62-5e59-80be-54a6ff21b8df" "answer-5a31e712-a209-5042-b075-583afd705df4" "answer-ce35cb8f-0474-597d-bfd4-76d275162807" "answer-363cf81e-b814-5bae-b21a-c5905092ae85"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a150de1e-a1ab-51fb-b47b-78fa9b93d0f2" :answer/id #uuid "a150de1e-a1ab-51fb-b47b-78fa9b93d0f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a0ba0b0-2f62-5e59-80be-54a6ff21b8df" :answer/id #uuid "0a0ba0b0-2f62-5e59-80be-54a6ff21b8df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a31e712-a209-5042-b075-583afd705df4" :answer/id #uuid "5a31e712-a209-5042-b075-583afd705df4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce35cb8f-0474-597d-bfd4-76d275162807" :answer/id #uuid "ce35cb8f-0474-597d-bfd4-76d275162807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-363cf81e-b814-5bae-b21a-c5905092ae85" :answer/id #uuid "363cf81e-b814-5bae-b21a-c5905092ae85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-72a8e42a-c396-5007-82f3-4a7623931a98" :question/id #uuid "72a8e42a-c396-5007-82f3-4a7623931a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-140949" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a46f04e1-116c-5ed7-918a-7a2a64582678"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a46f04e1-116c-5ed7-918a-7a2a64582678" :answer-field/id #uuid "a46f04e1-116c-5ed7-918a-7a2a64582678" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-389c2900-a592-57ae-bfa1-7bbef48b53d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-62a63059-3b74-51be-aa83-8b1410096d19" "answer-389c2900-a592-57ae-bfa1-7bbef48b53d1" "answer-2287b3ed-5a16-5b93-98d5-92c303dcba81" "answer-0afb3631-bd9c-5f23-83f1-de3a65e2c41a" "answer-dfe8354a-f4d5-505e-8c20-63690bd7fa8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62a63059-3b74-51be-aa83-8b1410096d19" :answer/id #uuid "62a63059-3b74-51be-aa83-8b1410096d19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-389c2900-a592-57ae-bfa1-7bbef48b53d1" :answer/id #uuid "389c2900-a592-57ae-bfa1-7bbef48b53d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2287b3ed-5a16-5b93-98d5-92c303dcba81" :answer/id #uuid "2287b3ed-5a16-5b93-98d5-92c303dcba81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0afb3631-bd9c-5f23-83f1-de3a65e2c41a" :answer/id #uuid "0afb3631-bd9c-5f23-83f1-de3a65e2c41a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfe8354a-f4d5-505e-8c20-63690bd7fa8e" :answer/id #uuid "dfe8354a-f4d5-505e-8c20-63690bd7fa8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22478d60-239d-58ef-b70b-d84ab0dcc7e7" :question/id #uuid "22478d60-239d-58ef-b70b-d84ab0dcc7e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40893" :question/problem "Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the least-squares solution of $Ax=b$, where \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-650f85f5-ea3b-575b-8153-eb659d02002b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-650f85f5-ea3b-575b-8153-eb659d02002b" :answer-field/id #uuid "650f85f5-ea3b-575b-8153-eb659d02002b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9b8962f3-5246-5106-b46e-ea24bd51177e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d9c66629-0b41-5749-a012-970816c45a65" "answer-9b8962f3-5246-5106-b46e-ea24bd51177e" "answer-81d718db-f9f0-5955-8716-95f339e2bec9" "answer-a1cb91f8-23b5-52f4-b2ba-fcdf27a4db28" "answer-0e63215d-e6da-5f48-8089-bb66c2440c78"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9c66629-0b41-5749-a012-970816c45a65" :answer/id #uuid "d9c66629-0b41-5749-a012-970816c45a65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b8962f3-5246-5106-b46e-ea24bd51177e" :answer/id #uuid "9b8962f3-5246-5106-b46e-ea24bd51177e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81d718db-f9f0-5955-8716-95f339e2bec9" :answer/id #uuid "81d718db-f9f0-5955-8716-95f339e2bec9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1cb91f8-23b5-52f4-b2ba-fcdf27a4db28" :answer/id #uuid "a1cb91f8-23b5-52f4-b2ba-fcdf27a4db28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{11} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e63215d-e6da-5f48-8089-bb66c2440c78" :answer/id #uuid "0e63215d-e6da-5f48-8089-bb66c2440c78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{11} \\\\ \\frac{1}{18}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326909 :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366139 :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-f16e5a65-b3a9-5e9e-afd2-4d31b6ccc499" :question/id #uuid "f16e5a65-b3a9-5e9e-afd2-4d31b6ccc499" :question/math-academy-id "q-140950" :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}[\\begin{aligned}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d397a581-a8a2-563e-b417-727d29b898d2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d397a581-a8a2-563e-b417-727d29b898d2" :answer-field/id #uuid "d397a581-a8a2-563e-b417-727d29b898d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ccdc69d-91d5-5343-83df-36f8554b4e24" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45e43c42-5da8-5d53-b35b-31833e6652cf" "answer-f999943f-8b6f-536c-a730-19ee3d37474d" "answer-0ccdc69d-91d5-5343-83df-36f8554b4e24" "answer-d2463526-c5e0-57c9-b136-c49c265fa1d9" "answer-26cb13db-58f4-5be0-b4aa-1bfd588cd45c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45e43c42-5da8-5d53-b35b-31833e6652cf" :answer/id #uuid "45e43c42-5da8-5d53-b35b-31833e6652cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f999943f-8b6f-536c-a730-19ee3d37474d" :answer/id #uuid "f999943f-8b6f-536c-a730-19ee3d37474d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ccdc69d-91d5-5343-83df-36f8554b4e24" :answer/id #uuid "0ccdc69d-91d5-5343-83df-36f8554b4e24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2463526-c5e0-57c9-b136-c49c265fa1d9" :answer/id #uuid "d2463526-c5e0-57c9-b136-c49c265fa1d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26cb13db-58f4-5be0-b4aa-1bfd588cd45c" :answer/id #uuid "26cb13db-58f4-5be0-b4aa-1bfd588cd45c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186392422 :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186394717 :question/problem "Find the least-squares solution to the system of linear equations given below. You're given that the columns of the corresponding coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-bbdebe64-e032-577f-8298-161c9dc3fe78" :question/id #uuid "bbdebe64-e032-577f-8298-161c9dc3fe78" :question/math-academy-id "q-85285" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are shown below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}}=\\sqrt{35}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-791b26ae-4f7f-58a8-b298-28b8f970263b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-791b26ae-4f7f-58a8-b298-28b8f970263b" :answer-field/id #uuid "791b26ae-4f7f-58a8-b298-28b8f970263b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-164af3d4-d329-5a63-9544-443eea1cc4f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8eaa36ae-b43e-5ed4-8193-ae54fff3942f" "answer-e5ce1773-5c8b-5827-b29e-4b8dba8f0126" "answer-164af3d4-d329-5a63-9544-443eea1cc4f5" "answer-2daf4fd5-f4c3-5cde-bed6-b74154aa47b7" "answer-2bbf719c-8336-5b63-8170-1187e7f4e55b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8eaa36ae-b43e-5ed4-8193-ae54fff3942f" :answer/id #uuid "8eaa36ae-b43e-5ed4-8193-ae54fff3942f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5ce1773-5c8b-5827-b29e-4b8dba8f0126" :answer/id #uuid "e5ce1773-5c8b-5827-b29e-4b8dba8f0126" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-164af3d4-d329-5a63-9544-443eea1cc4f5" :answer/id #uuid "164af3d4-d329-5a63-9544-443eea1cc4f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{35}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2daf4fd5-f4c3-5cde-bed6-b74154aa47b7" :answer/id #uuid "2daf4fd5-f4c3-5cde-bed6-b74154aa47b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bbf719c-8336-5b63-8170-1187e7f4e55b" :answer/id #uuid "2bbf719c-8336-5b63-8170-1187e7f4e55b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135059 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is easy to show that the system is inconsistent (i.e., it has no solution). However, in real-world problems, we might want to find an approximate solution to the system. \n\nLet's formulate the problem a little more rigorously. Firstly, we write down the system in the form $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/22/22b8abd4638e579875755f482442cf1dbf89653bd9d396b4a802217e25177839.svg)\n\nWe are interested in vectors $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ such that $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ is very close to $\\mathbf b.$ More precisely, we wish to *minimize the distance* between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b.$ Recall that the distance between $A\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ and $\\mathbf b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nThe smaller the value of $\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert,$ the better our approximation $\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}$ will be. \n\nIn terms of vector subspaces, we wish to find a vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and is closest to $\\mathbf{b}.$\nTherefore, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ must be the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nIt can be shown that ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is found by solving the corresponding **normal equation**, given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this at the end of the lesson. Note that the normal equation is obtained by left-multiplying the equation $A {\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = \\mathbf b$ by $A^T.$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix $A^T \\! A$ is invertible. Therefore, left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T \\! A)^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} = (A^T \\! A)^{-1}A^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 18\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{99}[\\begin{aligned}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{33} \\\\ -\\frac{38}{33}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is called the **least-squares solution** of the given system.\n\n**Watch out!** We assumed that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, which guarantees that $A^T \\! A\\,$ is invertible. However, if the columns of $A$ are linearly *dependent,* then we have to use a different approach to solve the problem." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186126643 :answer-field/choices 17592186126644] [:db/retract 17592186126643 :answer-field/choices 17592186126645] [:db/retract 17592186126643 :answer-field/choices 17592186126646] [:db/retract 17592186126643 :answer-field/choices 17592186126647] [:db/retract 17592186126643 :answer-field/choices 17592186126648] [:db/retract 17592186128029 :answer-field/choices 17592186128032] [:db/retract 17592186128029 :answer-field/choices 17592186128033] [:db/retract 17592186128029 :answer-field/choices 17592186128034] [:db/retract 17592186128029 :answer-field/choices 17592186128030] [:db/retract 17592186128029 :answer-field/choices 17592186128031] [:db/retract 17592186128047 :answer-field/choices 17592186128048] [:db/retract 17592186128047 :answer-field/choices 17592186128049] [:db/retract 17592186128047 :answer-field/choices 17592186128050] [:db/retract 17592186128047 :answer-field/choices 17592186128051] [:db/retract 17592186128047 :answer-field/choices 17592186128052] {:db/id 17592186129264 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac302ee6-6bbe-5388-bf60-4247113c7536" "question-b6dfd2f6-e989-5dc0-8a5f-19807faa0173" "question-31ed4558-6b89-5259-81f7-c1113b514f8f" "question-1521c62d-edb7-5cf4-9412-1fda40e8b968" "question-80477a9d-e7af-5d9c-aba6-cf99ec36f94b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129281 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4a047214-1415-5946-9ec9-da29ccac571a" "question-ca85c537-9f01-5979-bc36-583972546691" "question-bbdf7ac8-9c8d-5122-94b8-607404da611a" "question-164ec461-433f-5d34-8b46-e1e7c87d7fe4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129265 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-75e6fbad-2ffa-554a-b73e-206d1de35163" "question-ba16213a-d9f2-5675-b515-f66161bd7267" "question-e9ac6b1f-2b21-59f9-9ae8-01d7811a1b26" "question-934f832d-975f-5cb5-827f-5f4ce2444543"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129266 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0136e908-03ab-5353-9218-7031e36c58ca" "question-4913e4d1-197e-5ba9-a574-72530dddbf74" "question-ddc59e97-e243-5e27-9d57-5e0f9b714882"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac302ee6-6bbe-5388-bf60-4247113c7536" :question/id #uuid "ac302ee6-6bbe-5388-bf60-4247113c7536" :question/math-academy-id "q-161750" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a40abc4f-2947-5476-abce-6432706d5eb5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a40abc4f-2947-5476-abce-6432706d5eb5" :answer-field/id #uuid "a40abc4f-2947-5476-abce-6432706d5eb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5f3113d8-4993-57ed-9b8e-75b1863a90d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0dcb9cd7-9763-5072-aea1-40956674c5d4" "answer-f8d5ffdc-8a6e-57db-9529-bc88c792c05e" "answer-c5b152c3-3ea3-55b4-aa0c-ac2b236c1936" "answer-3f89b472-b769-5a09-b612-61bb4b463d5e" "answer-5f3113d8-4993-57ed-9b8e-75b1863a90d5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0dcb9cd7-9763-5072-aea1-40956674c5d4" :answer/id #uuid "0dcb9cd7-9763-5072-aea1-40956674c5d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8d5ffdc-8a6e-57db-9529-bc88c792c05e" :answer/id #uuid "f8d5ffdc-8a6e-57db-9529-bc88c792c05e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5b152c3-3ea3-55b4-aa0c-ac2b236c1936" :answer/id #uuid "c5b152c3-3ea3-55b4-aa0c-ac2b236c1936" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f89b472-b769-5a09-b612-61bb4b463d5e" :answer/id #uuid "3f89b472-b769-5a09-b612-61bb4b463d5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f3113d8-4993-57ed-9b8e-75b1863a90d5" :answer/id #uuid "5f3113d8-4993-57ed-9b8e-75b1863a90d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b6dfd2f6-e989-5dc0-8a5f-19807faa0173" :question/id #uuid "b6dfd2f6-e989-5dc0-8a5f-19807faa0173" :question/math-academy-id "q-161743" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-747ee5c7-5db1-5a10-9753-ab1d396bd128"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-747ee5c7-5db1-5a10-9753-ab1d396bd128" :answer-field/id #uuid "747ee5c7-5db1-5a10-9753-ab1d396bd128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-32cfd7a5-0554-5c53-becb-3f321129eb74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b9b0a73-84b0-590f-9a4e-7f5d19046ccf" "answer-32cfd7a5-0554-5c53-becb-3f321129eb74" "answer-370eb795-4874-5cf9-8f5c-cdd4a06415a3" "answer-bb855a55-4eb5-563e-aef7-ff1f60e275c1" "answer-cad5272c-2c13-5856-85fe-8cf9637d07f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b9b0a73-84b0-590f-9a4e-7f5d19046ccf" :answer/id #uuid "3b9b0a73-84b0-590f-9a4e-7f5d19046ccf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32cfd7a5-0554-5c53-becb-3f321129eb74" :answer/id #uuid "32cfd7a5-0554-5c53-becb-3f321129eb74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-370eb795-4874-5cf9-8f5c-cdd4a06415a3" :answer/id #uuid "370eb795-4874-5cf9-8f5c-cdd4a06415a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb855a55-4eb5-563e-aef7-ff1f60e275c1" :answer/id #uuid "bb855a55-4eb5-563e-aef7-ff1f60e275c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cad5272c-2c13-5856-85fe-8cf9637d07f1" :answer/id #uuid "cad5272c-2c13-5856-85fe-8cf9637d07f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-31ed4558-6b89-5259-81f7-c1113b514f8f" :question/id #uuid "31ed4558-6b89-5259-81f7-c1113b514f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161755" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xz({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-34e8d1a7-8446-506e-a289-5e07cc9420e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34e8d1a7-8446-506e-a289-5e07cc9420e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "34e8d1a7-8446-506e-a289-5e07cc9420e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f40a7aeb-b349-5ecd-b0c6-8c4dfad465cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea0c92ac-4a7e-5612-8b1a-cdab04a75fc5" "answer-6d4efdf4-7132-5a7c-902a-3084f4ac7da4" "answer-f40a7aeb-b349-5ecd-b0c6-8c4dfad465cc" "answer-9dc3a237-3a9b-5a3e-86b6-46c598ab506a" "answer-dcecb712-fe3e-501f-918d-8a68c107c94f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea0c92ac-4a7e-5612-8b1a-cdab04a75fc5" :answer/id #uuid "ea0c92ac-4a7e-5612-8b1a-cdab04a75fc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d4efdf4-7132-5a7c-902a-3084f4ac7da4" :answer/id #uuid "6d4efdf4-7132-5a7c-902a-3084f4ac7da4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f40a7aeb-b349-5ecd-b0c6-8c4dfad465cc" :answer/id #uuid "f40a7aeb-b349-5ecd-b0c6-8c4dfad465cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dc3a237-3a9b-5a3e-86b6-46c598ab506a" :answer/id #uuid "9dc3a237-3a9b-5a3e-86b6-46c598ab506a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcecb712-fe3e-501f-918d-8a68c107c94f" :answer/id #uuid "dcecb712-fe3e-501f-918d-8a68c107c94f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{6}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1521c62d-edb7-5cf4-9412-1fda40e8b968" :question/id #uuid "1521c62d-edb7-5cf4-9412-1fda40e8b968" :question/math-academy-id "q-161758" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x+y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x+y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x+y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6c56d916-834b-5bb9-948e-1a1744f8d9b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6c56d916-834b-5bb9-948e-1a1744f8d9b7" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c56d916-834b-5bb9-948e-1a1744f8d9b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-15050ad1-bbf7-5ae7-b969-2f07d4306ebd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7127edd8-9885-5717-9356-bf13d8c61bbe" "answer-92f38256-28ff-58e2-87f8-76d91c5a2b42" "answer-15050ad1-bbf7-5ae7-b969-2f07d4306ebd" "answer-801036a5-3ed7-528a-92f4-c8ef0bebef28" "answer-6488d04a-03fe-5654-a722-67d715654669"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7127edd8-9885-5717-9356-bf13d8c61bbe" :answer/id #uuid "7127edd8-9885-5717-9356-bf13d8c61bbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92f38256-28ff-58e2-87f8-76d91c5a2b42" :answer/id #uuid "92f38256-28ff-58e2-87f8-76d91c5a2b42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15050ad1-bbf7-5ae7-b969-2f07d4306ebd" :answer/id #uuid "15050ad1-bbf7-5ae7-b969-2f07d4306ebd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-801036a5-3ed7-528a-92f4-c8ef0bebef28" :answer/id #uuid "801036a5-3ed7-528a-92f4-c8ef0bebef28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6488d04a-03fe-5654-a722-67d715654669" :answer/id #uuid "6488d04a-03fe-5654-a722-67d715654669" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-80477a9d-e7af-5d9c-aba6-cf99ec36f94b" :question/id #uuid "80477a9d-e7af-5d9c-aba6-cf99ec36f94b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161762" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $6$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =((\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2})\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc1dcc32-b424-50ae-8bc7-6bf9e3e11130"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc1dcc32-b424-50ae-8bc7-6bf9e3e11130" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1dcc32-b424-50ae-8bc7-6bf9e3e11130" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f53097bb-f59d-547e-b750-92460571ca57" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9172fead-91e9-5a66-a2fa-3f44604487dd" "answer-c77d6454-6af7-5b8b-a02e-7768e6bb2a38" "answer-127543e8-793d-5a57-a8aa-34e7946706c3" "answer-1e3c2c33-40a9-5bfb-8e72-a111146071ce" "answer-f53097bb-f59d-547e-b750-92460571ca57"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9172fead-91e9-5a66-a2fa-3f44604487dd" :answer/id #uuid "9172fead-91e9-5a66-a2fa-3f44604487dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{36}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c77d6454-6af7-5b8b-a02e-7768e6bb2a38" :answer/id #uuid "c77d6454-6af7-5b8b-a02e-7768e6bb2a38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-127543e8-793d-5a57-a8aa-34e7946706c3" :answer/id #uuid "127543e8-793d-5a57-a8aa-34e7946706c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{36}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e3c2c33-40a9-5bfb-8e72-a111146071ce" :answer/id #uuid "1e3c2c33-40a9-5bfb-8e72-a111146071ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f53097bb-f59d-547e-b750-92460571ca57" :answer/id #uuid "f53097bb-f59d-547e-b750-92460571ca57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6}{\\rho }^{5}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a047214-1415-5946-9ec9-da29ccac571a" :question/id #uuid "4a047214-1415-5946-9ec9-da29ccac571a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161774" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6$ for $x\\le 0$ and $z\\le 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{6}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/02/02834e98afa14d5193e5d560f85457e73caee7f5938af5ac8b3fb607165aad53.png)\n\n \nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce36ee07-fbbc-5e29-8d0c-a6748c1dafd2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce36ee07-fbbc-5e29-8d0c-a6748c1dafd2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce36ee07-fbbc-5e29-8d0c-a6748c1dafd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3ad64f33-04da-536f-b19e-fda75bf79d7e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9ebb7ed-a4dc-5ae7-a75b-d082ec3f13f7" "answer-53891021-4964-5177-a087-40074c3388c2" "answer-bac58cc0-72ac-5a02-bb3a-4587ad3cce80" "answer-3ad64f33-04da-536f-b19e-fda75bf79d7e" "answer-5250dc04-dac8-5129-8673-b9a3ff91da85"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9ebb7ed-a4dc-5ae7-a75b-d082ec3f13f7" :answer/id #uuid "e9ebb7ed-a4dc-5ae7-a75b-d082ec3f13f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53891021-4964-5177-a087-40074c3388c2" :answer/id #uuid "53891021-4964-5177-a087-40074c3388c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bac58cc0-72ac-5a02-bb3a-4587ad3cce80" :answer/id #uuid "bac58cc0-72ac-5a02-bb3a-4587ad3cce80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{3}{sin}^{4}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ad64f33-04da-536f-b19e-fda75bf79d7e" :answer/id #uuid "3ad64f33-04da-536f-b19e-fda75bf79d7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5250dc04-dac8-5129-8673-b9a3ff91da85" :answer/id #uuid "5250dc04-dac8-5129-8673-b9a3ff91da85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6}}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{4}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129524 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $1$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/d4/d497c83f7eb77b0b0950f5a1c1f5d429574b2734743ef91bbdb57541f7f7d90f.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186126643 :answer-field/correct "answer-e9eb8b1d-e3e7-5472-b004-dc41500151c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9eb8b1d-e3e7-5472-b004-dc41500151c9" "answer-c3b0d3d7-1721-52c4-aa27-f26210c8570e" "answer-cffe5ce3-aa02-50c5-b565-9b545a47d290" "answer-b6f1f213-0804-5392-8e07-97dea35d05a6" "answer-0c7422a1-caf6-53c5-96d5-b8cc18e5e574"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9eb8b1d-e3e7-5472-b004-dc41500151c9" :answer/id #uuid "e9eb8b1d-e3e7-5472-b004-dc41500151c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3b0d3d7-1721-52c4-aa27-f26210c8570e" :answer/id #uuid "c3b0d3d7-1721-52c4-aa27-f26210c8570e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cffe5ce3-aa02-50c5-b565-9b545a47d290" :answer/id #uuid "cffe5ce3-aa02-50c5-b565-9b545a47d290" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6f1f213-0804-5392-8e07-97dea35d05a6" :answer/id #uuid "b6f1f213-0804-5392-8e07-97dea35d05a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c7422a1-caf6-53c5-96d5-b8cc18e5e574" :answer/id #uuid "0c7422a1-caf6-53c5-96d5-b8cc18e5e574" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca85c537-9f01-5979-bc36-583972546691" :question/id #uuid "ca85c537-9f01-5979-bc36-583972546691" :question/math-academy-id "q-161773" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ for $x\\le 0$ and $z\\ge 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/c4/c4d31af830ee7d166662e88b1a7ade0c817d2508b887c15db5838d25b9467fca.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & ={({\\rho }^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{4}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-798c1031-b470-536a-bb3e-eb04c8182884"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-798c1031-b470-536a-bb3e-eb04c8182884" :answer-field/id #uuid "798c1031-b470-536a-bb3e-eb04c8182884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-35fc13f0-b773-5726-937d-054c6171b474" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85067350-ec8f-5ce8-a536-413b150f3281" "answer-35fc13f0-b773-5726-937d-054c6171b474" "answer-7d57d3ba-c82f-5a22-baeb-bcab9c948a51" "answer-69680282-f956-58f9-9786-03a064080a79" "answer-c9d4b60c-7ed9-5434-80af-c8fd8eec46c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85067350-ec8f-5ce8-a536-413b150f3281" :answer/id #uuid "85067350-ec8f-5ce8-a536-413b150f3281" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35fc13f0-b773-5726-937d-054c6171b474" :answer/id #uuid "35fc13f0-b773-5726-937d-054c6171b474" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d57d3ba-c82f-5a22-baeb-bcab9c948a51" :answer/id #uuid "7d57d3ba-c82f-5a22-baeb-bcab9c948a51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{6}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69680282-f956-58f9-9786-03a064080a79" :answer/id #uuid "69680282-f956-58f9-9786-03a064080a79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :